window.B4COURSE = {"title": "Block 4 Academy — Electricity & Electronics", "subtitle": "Fundamentals of Electricity & Electronics · FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12", "passMark": 0.7, "storeKey": "block4-academy-v1", "modules": [{"n": 1, "day": "Unit 1", "title": "Electricity Basics: Atoms, Ohm's Law & Resistors", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Fundamentals · ACS AM.I.A.K1, K7a/c, K10, K11, K20", "sections": [{"id": "b4m1s1", "title": "Matter, atoms & electron theory", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The picture for the whole block.</b> Electricity in a wire behaves like\n<b>water in a pipe</b>. <b>Voltage is the pressure</b> pushing, <b>current is the\nflow</b> actually moving, and <b>resistance is a pinch</b> in the pipe fighting\nthe flow. We lean on that picture through all four units. But first — what is\nactually flowing? Electrons. So we start with the atom.</p>\n\n<h3>Matter, elements, compounds, molecules, atoms</h3>\n<p><b>Matter</b> is anything that has <b>mass and volume</b> — the stuff physical\nobjects are made of. It comes in four states: solid, liquid, gas, and plasma;\nas a technician you deal with the first three (FAA p. 12-1).</p>\n<p>The vocabulary ladder, smallest words first (all FAA p. 12-1):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <b>element</b> is a substance that <b>cannot be reduced to a simpler form by\nchemical means</b> — iron, gold, oxygen.</li>\n<li>A <b>compound</b> is a <b>chemical combination of two or more elements</b> —\nwater is two hydrogen atoms plus one oxygen atom.</li>\n<li>A <b>molecule</b> is the <b>smallest particle of a substance that still keeps\nthe identity of that substance</b> (the smallest bit that is still \"water\").</li>\n<li>The <b>atom</b> is the basic building block of all matter, made of protons,\nneutrons, and electrons.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Inside the atom</h3>\n<p><b>Analogy: a tiny solar system.</b> The <b>nucleus</b> (the Sun) holds the\n<b>protons (+)</b> and <b>neutrons (no charge)</b> (p. 12-1). The <b>electrons\n(−)</b> orbit like planets. The electron is a lightweight: a proton has about\n<b>1,837 times</b> the mass of an electron (p. 12-2).</p>\n<p>An atom is <b>NEUTRAL</b> when the positive proton charge <b>equals</b> the\nnegative electron charge — the plus and minus cancel (p. 12-2).</p>\n<p>Electrons live in <b>shells</b>, and each shell has a headcount limit given by\nthe equation <b>2n&sup2;</b> (n = the shell number): shell 1 holds 2, shell 2 holds\n8, shell 3 holds 18 (p. 12-2).</p>\n<p>The outermost shell is the <b>valence</b> shell, and <b>valence</b> is the\nnumber of chemical bonds an atom can form — set by the electrons in that outer\nshell (p. 12-2). The loosely-bound outer electrons that can drift from atom to\natom are the <b>free electrons</b> — they ARE the current (p. 12-2).</p>\n\n<h3>Ions — atoms that lost or gained</h3>\n<p>The process of an atom <b>losing or gaining electrons</b> is\n<b>ionization</b> (p. 12-2). Keep the two results straight:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Atom <b>LOSES</b> an electron → left positive → a <b>positive ion\n(cation)</b> (p. 12-2).</li>\n<li>Atom <b>GAINS</b> an electron → turns negative → a <b>negative ion\n(anion)</b> (p. 12-2).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Voltage, current, and the units</h3>\n<p><b>Voltage</b> (electromotive force, emf) is the electrical <b>pressure</b>\nthat moves electrons. Its formula symbol is <b>E</b>, and it is measured in <b>volts</b>\n(p. 12-14). And here's a subtlety the exam likes: voltage is always\n<b>determined between TWO points</b> — it's a difference in pressure, like\ncomparing two ends of a pipe (p. 12-14).</p>\n<p><b>Current</b> is the <b>flow</b> — electrons on the move. Formula symbol\n<b>I</b>, unit the <b>ampere</b> (p. 12-16). One ampere = <b>1 coulomb of charge\npassing a point in one second</b>, and one coulomb is about <b>6.28 billion\nbillion (6.28 × 10¹⁸) electrons</b> (p. 12-17). When the current flows in only\n<b>one direction</b>, it's <b>direct current (DC)</b> (p. 12-17).</p>\n<p>One more term: individual electrons actually creep along the wire slowly —\nthat slow average crawl toward the positive end is the <b>drift velocity</b>\n(p. 12-17). (The <i>effect</i> travels near light speed — like a pipe already\nfull of water: push one end, water pops out the other instantly.)</p>\n\n<h3>Which way does current \"flow\"? Two conventions</h3>\n<p><b>Conventional current</b> — Benjamin Franklin's assumption — says current\nflows <b>from positive (+) to negative (−)</b> (p. 12-14). Later physics showed\nthe electrons really move the other way. The FAA officially defines current as\n<b>ELECTRON FLOW: from negative to positive</b> (p. 12-14). Worksheets and the\nwritten both test this pair — know both, and know which one the FAA uses.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Matter → element (can't be chemically simplified) → compound (2+ elements) →\nmolecule (smallest piece that's still the substance) → atom (p. 12-1).</li>\n<li>Nucleus = protons + neutrons; proton mass ≈ <b>1,837×</b> the electron; neutral\natom = proton charge <b>equals</b> electron charge (p. 12-1/12-2).</li>\n<li>Shell capacity = <b>2n&sup2;</b>. Outer shell = valence shell; its drifters =\n<b>free electrons</b> (p. 12-2).</li>\n<li>Ionization: lose an electron = <b>positive ion (cation)</b>; gain one =\n<b>negative ion (anion)</b> (p. 12-2).</li>\n<li><b>E</b> = voltage, in volts, between two points. <b>I</b> = current, in amperes.\n1 amp = 1 coulomb/second = 6.28 billion billion electrons/second (p. 12-14…12-17).</li>\n<li>DC = one-direction current; slow electron creep = <b>drift velocity</b> (p. 12-17).</li>\n<li>Conventional flow = + to − (Franklin). <b>FAA official = electron flow, − to +</b>\n(p. 12-14).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Matter, atomic structure, ions, emf, current), pp. 12-1…12-17.</p>\n", "topics": ["Matter & Atoms", "Conventional vs Electron Flow", "EMF, Current & Units"], "quiz": [{"q": "The conventional current theory, advanced by Benjamin Franklin, holds that current flows:", "options": ["From positive (+) to negative (-)", "In both directions at once", "Only in AC circuits", "From negative to positive"], "correct": 0, "short": "Positive to negative", "expl": "Conventional current theory, advanced by Franklin, visualizes current as flowing from positive (+) to negative (-). Electron flow is the opposite.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Conventional vs Electron Flow"}, {"q": "An atom is said to have a NEUTRAL charge when:", "options": ["It has more protons than electrons", "It contains only neutrons", "It has more electrons than protons", "The positive proton charge equals the negative electron charge"], "correct": 3, "short": "Proton (+) charge = electron (-) charge", "expl": "When the total positive charge of the protons in the nucleus equals the total negative charge of the orbiting electrons, the atom has a neutral charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "The maximum number of electrons that any shell can hold is defined by the equation:", "options": ["n^2", "n/2", "2n", "2n^2"], "correct": 3, "short": "2n^2", "expl": "Electron capacity = 2n^2, where n is the energy level. The first shell holds 2 electrons, the second 8, the third 18, and so on.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "An atom that has LOST an electron becomes a positively charged particle called a:", "options": ["Free electron", "Anion", "Neutron", "Positive ion (cation)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Positive ion (cation)", "expl": "Dislodging an electron leaves the atom net positive — a positive ion or cation. An atom that GAINS an electron becomes a negative ion or anion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-2", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}, {"q": "How does the FAA officially define the direction of current flow?", "options": ["Electron flow (negative to positive)", "Conventional flow (positive to negative)", "Bidirectional flow", "Drift flow"], "correct": 0, "short": "Electron flow (negative to positive)", "expl": "The FAA officially defines current flow using electron theory: negative to positive. Electron flow is what actually happens — electrons flow from the negative source to the positive.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Conventional vs Electron Flow"}, {"q": "One ampere (A) of current is equivalent to how much charge passing a point in one second?", "options": ["1 joule", "1 ohm", "1 volt", "1 coulomb"], "correct": 3, "short": "1 coulomb", "expl": "1 A = 1 coulomb per second. One ampere equals one coulomb of charge passing through a conductor in one second.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "EMF, Current & Units"}, {"q": "The relatively slow average velocity at which electrons drift toward the positive end of a conductor is called the:", "options": ["Drift velocity", "Phase velocity", "Escape velocity", "Saturation velocity"], "correct": 0, "short": "Drift velocity", "expl": "The average velocity of the charge is called drift velocity — a relatively slow speed — even though the effect of current is nearly instantaneous.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "EMF, Current & Units"}, {"q": "Electrons in motion making up an electric current are measured as a rate of charge flow. What is the symbol used for current in formulas?", "options": ["R", "I", "Q", "E"], "correct": 1, "short": "I", "expl": "The symbol used to indicate current in formulas or on schematics is the capital letter 'I'. Current is a measure of the rate at which charge flows.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-16", "topic": "EMF, Current & Units"}, {"q": "The most basic building block of all matter, composed of protons, neutrons, and electrons, is the:", "options": ["Cell", "Compound", "Molecule", "Atom"], "correct": 3, "short": "Atom", "expl": "The atom is the most basic building block of all matter and is composed of three subatomic particles: protons, neutrons, and electrons.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-1", "topic": "Matter & Atoms"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s2", "title": "Static electricity & ESD", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Rub a balloon on your hair and it sticks to the wall.\nThat's electricity with the flow stopped — charge sitting still, waiting.\nFor an aircraft mechanic, static isn't a party trick: it's a component-killer\nand a refueling hazard, which is why the FAA gives it its own section.</p>\n\n<h3>What static electricity is</h3>\n<p><b>Static electricity</b> is electricity in which the electrons are <b>at\nrest</b> — a charge built up on the surface of an object, with no current\nflowing (p. 12-4). Rub two materials together and electrons jump from one to\nthe other: the loser goes positive, the gainer goes negative.</p>\n<p>The fundamental law of static electricity (and of magnetism, as you'll see in\nUnit 3): <b>like charges repel, unlike charges attract</b> (p. 12-4).</p>\n\n<h3>The electrostatic field</h3>\n<p>Around every charged body there's a <b>field of force</b>, drawn as lines\nextending in all directions: the <b>electrostatic field</b> (also called the\n<b>dielectric field</b>) (p. 12-5). By arbitrary agreement, the field's\n<b>direction</b> is determined using a small <b>positive test charge</b>: the\ndirection the little + charge would be pushed is the direction of the field\n(p. 12-5).</p>\n<p>Shape matters. On an irregularly-shaped charged object, the charge does NOT\nspread evenly — <b>the greatest charge concentrates at the points, or areas of\nsharpest curvature</b> (p. 12-5). That's why lightning rods and static wicks are\npointy: sharp points bleed charge off first.</p>\n\n<h3>ESD — the silent component-killer</h3>\n<p><b>Electrostatic discharge (ESD)</b> is one of the <b>most frequent causes of\ndamage to solid-state components and integrated circuits</b> during handling\n(p. 12-5). Walking across carpet can charge YOU to thousands of volts. Touch an\navionics board and that charge dumps through it — often wounding the chip\ninvisibly so it fails later.</p>\n<p>The fix: before and while handling solid-state parts, <b>wear a grounding\nwrist strap and discharge your body's static</b> to ground (p. 12-5). Handle\nboards by the edges, keep them in anti-static bags.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">On the aircraft</span>\nThe same physics is why airframes carry bonding straps and pointy static\ndischarge wicks on the trailing edges — they give accumulated charge a\ncontrolled exit instead of letting it arc through structure or radios. You'll\nmeet those hands-on in the Airframe section of the program.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Static electricity = electrons <b>at rest</b>; a surface charge, no current (p. 12-4).</li>\n<li><b>Like charges repel; unlike charges attract</b> (p. 12-4).</li>\n<li>The field around a charged body = <b>electrostatic (dielectric) field</b>;\ndirection is defined with a small <b>positive test charge</b> (p. 12-5).</li>\n<li>Greatest charge concentrates at the <b>points of sharpest curvature</b> (p. 12-5).</li>\n<li><b>ESD</b> = a most-frequent cause of solid-state/IC damage; protect with a\n<b>grounding wrist strap</b> + discharged body (p. 12-5).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Static electricity, electrostatic field, ESD), pp. 12-4…12-5.</p>\n", "topics": ["Static Electricity"], "quiz": [{"q": "On an irregularly-shaped charged object, the greatest charge concentrates at:", "options": ["The center", "The areas of least curvature", "The flat surfaces", "The points or areas of sharpest curvature"], "correct": 3, "short": "The points / sharpest curvature", "expl": "The charge on an irregularly-shaped object is not evenly distributed; the greatest charge is at the points, or areas of sharpest curvature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "Per the fundamental law of static electricity (and magnetism), how do charges behave?", "options": ["All charges attract", "All charges repel", "Like charges attract; unlike charges repel", "Like charges repel; unlike charges attract"], "correct": 3, "short": "Like charges repel; unlike charges attract", "expl": "One of the most fundamental laws of static electricity and magnetism: like charges repel each other and unlike charges attract each other.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "To safely protect a solid-state component from ESD while handling it, a technician should:", "options": ["Wear a grounding wrist strap and discharge body static", "Hold it by the pins", "Keep it in direct sunlight", "Warm it before handling"], "correct": 0, "short": "Wear a grounding wrist strap", "expl": "ESD damage is avoided by discharging body static (touch the chassis of the removed box), wearing a grounding wrist strap, and good professional handling.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "The field of force that exists around a charged body, represented by lines extending in all directions, is called the:", "options": ["Electrostatic (dielectric) field", "Magnetic field", "Eddy field", "Gravitational field"], "correct": 0, "short": "Electrostatic (dielectric) field", "expl": "A field of force around a charged body is an electrostatic field (sometimes called a dielectric field), represented by lines extending in all directions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "By arbitrary agreement, the direction of an electrostatic field is determined using a small:", "options": ["Positive test charge", "Magnetic pole", "Negative test charge", "Neutral test charge"], "correct": 0, "short": "Positive test charge", "expl": "It is arbitrarily agreed that a small positive test charge is used to determine field direction; the field around a positive charge points away from it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "One of the most frequent causes of damage to a solid-state component or integrated circuit when handled is:", "options": ["Electrostatic discharge (ESD)", "High humidity", "Low voltage", "Magnetic interference"], "correct": 0, "short": "Electrostatic discharge (ESD)", "expl": "ESD from the human body is one of the most frequent causes of damage to solid-state components. ESD-sensitive parts ship in special protective bags.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-5", "topic": "Static Electricity"}, {"q": "Electricity in which the electrons are at rest (a buildup of charge on the surface of an object, with no current flowing) is called:", "options": ["Alternating current", "Static electricity", "Dynamic electricity", "Direct current"], "correct": 1, "short": "Static electricity", "expl": "Static electricity is a buildup of charge on the surface of an object; it is 'static' because no current is flowing. Dynamic electricity has electrons in motion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Static Electricity"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s3", "title": "Conductors, wire & sources of electricity", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Whether electrons flow easily is decided by the valence\nshell you met last section: <b>fewer than 4 valence electrons → conductor;\nmore than 4 → insulator</b>. This section is the parts catalog: which materials\nconduct, how wire is sized, what makes resistance, and where voltage comes from.</p>\n\n<h3>Conductors, insulators, semiconductors</h3>\n<p>The best conductors are the metals with the most free electrons:\n<b>gold, copper, and silver</b> lead the list (p. 12-3). For a given wire\ndiameter, <b>copper is usually considered the best available conductor</b> —\nsilver is slightly better but too expensive (p. 12-18). <b>Aluminum</b> gets the\njob when <b>weight (and cost)</b> matter — it's much lighter than copper, which\nis why big transport-aircraft feeder cables are aluminum (p. 12-18).</p>\n<p><b>Insulators</b> — glass, ceramic, plastic — don't conduct because under\nnormal conditions their atoms <b>do not produce free electrons</b> (p. 12-3).\nPut an insulator in an extremely strong field, though, and its outer electrons\ncan be torn loose — a usually damaging event called <b>breakdown</b> (p. 12-4).</p>\n<p>In between sit the <b>semiconductors</b> — <b>silicon and germanium</b> —\nthe two materials transistors and integrated circuits are built from (p. 12-4).\nUnit 4 is full of them.</p>\n\n<h3>Metric prefixes (you'll use these constantly)</h3>\n<p><b>kilo (k)</b> = one thousand; <b>mega (M)</b> = one million; <b>milli (m)</b>\n= one-thousandth; <b>micro (µ)</b> = <b>one-millionth</b> (p. 12-4). So 2 kΩ =\n2,000 ohms and 10 mA = 0.010 amp.</p>\n\n<h3>What sets a wire's resistance — the pinch in the pipe</h3>\n<p>Resistance is measured in <b>ohms</b>, symbol <b>the Greek letter omega\n(Ω)</b> (p. 12-18). Four factors set a conductor's resistance: <b>material,\nlength, cross-sectional area, and temperature</b> (p. 12-19). Think pipe:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Longer pipe = more pinch.</b> Resistance is <b>directly proportional to\nlength</b> (p. 12-18).</li>\n<li><b>Fatter pipe = less pinch.</b> Resistance is inversely proportional to\ncross-sectional area — <b>double the area, cut resistance in half</b> (p. 12-19).</li>\n<li><b>Hotter metal = more pinch.</b> For most conductor materials, resistance\n<b>increases as temperature rises</b> (p. 12-19). The famous exception:\n<b>carbon</b>, whose resistance <b>decreases</b> as it heats (p. 12-19). The\namount a 1-ohm sample rises per degree above 0 °C is the material's\n<b>temperature coefficient of resistance</b> (p. 12-19).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Wire gauge (AWG) — the backwards numbers</h3>\n<p>Aircraft wire is sized by <b>American Wire Gauge</b>, and the numbers run\nbackwards: <b>bigger gauge number = smaller wire</b>. Wire dimensions use the\n<b>mil</b> — <b>one-thousandth of an inch</b> (p. 12-20). Three ratios to\nmemorize (all p. 12-20):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each <b>decrease of ONE</b> gauge number ≈ <b>25 percent increase</b> in\ncross-sectional area.</li>\n<li>A decrease of <b>THREE</b> gauge numbers ≈ doubles the area — and <b>cuts the\nresistance in half</b> for a given length.</li>\n<li>A change of <b>TEN</b> gauge numbers ≈ a <b>10:1</b> change in area.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Where voltage comes from</h3>\n<p>Besides friction (static) and magnetism (generators — Unit 3), the handbook\nlists the <b>four most common methods of producing electrical energy</b>:\n<b>pressure, chemical, thermal, and light</b> (p. 12-22). Each has a name worth\nknowing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pressure → piezoelectric effect:</b> squeeze a crystal such as quartz and\nit produces a voltage (p. 12-22).</li>\n<li><b>Heat → Seebeck effect:</b> a <b>thermocouple</b> — a junction of two\ndissimilar metals — generates a voltage proportional to its temperature. That's\nyour cylinder-head-temp and EGT gauges (p. 12-23).</li>\n<li><b>Light → solar (photovoltaic) cell:</b> converts light energy directly\ninto electricity (p. 12-23).</li>\n<li><b>Chemical → the battery</b> (all of Unit 3, Section 3).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Best conductors: <b>gold, copper, silver</b> (free electrons); copper = best\npractical for a given diameter; <b>aluminum when weight/cost</b> rule (p. 12-3, 12-18).</li>\n<li>Insulators (glass/ceramic/plastic) have <b>no free electrons</b>; forced\nconduction = <b>breakdown</b>; semiconductors = <b>silicon & germanium</b> (p. 12-3/12-4).</li>\n<li>k = 1,000 · M = 1,000,000 · m = 1/1,000 · <b>µ = one-millionth</b> (p. 12-4).</li>\n<li>Resistance (ohm, <b>omega Ω</b>): four factors = <b>material, length, area,\ntemperature</b>; ∝ length, inverse to area (double area = half R); rises with\ntemperature — <b>except carbon</b>; per-degree rise = <b>temperature coefficient</b> (p. 12-18/12-19).</li>\n<li>AWG: 1 mil = 0.001 inch; −1 gauge ≈ +25% area; <b>−3 gauge = half the\nresistance</b>; 10 gauge numbers = <b>10:1 area</b> (p. 12-20).</li>\n<li>Four common sources: <b>pressure (piezoelectric), chemical (battery), thermal\n(Seebeck/thermocouple), light (photovoltaic cell)</b> (p. 12-22/12-23).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Conductors/insulators, units, wire, sources of electricity), pp. 12-3…12-23.</p>\n", "topics": ["Conductors & Insulators", "Conductors & Wire", "Sources of Electricity", "Metric Prefixes"], "quiz": [{"q": "Producing a voltage by applying mechanical pressure to a crystal such as quartz is called the:", "options": ["Seebeck effect", "Hall effect", "Piezoelectric effect", "Photovoltaic effect"], "correct": 2, "short": "Piezoelectric effect", "expl": "Piezoelectric generation results from mechanical pressure on a dielectric crystal (e.g., quartz, Rochelle salt). The crystal produces a voltage proportional to the stress.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-22", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "In wire measurement standards used in North America, one 'mil' equals:", "options": ["One inch", "One-thousandth of an inch", "One-tenth of an inch", "One-hundredth of an inch"], "correct": 1, "short": "One-thousandth of an inch", "expl": "The mil (one-thousandth of an inch) is the unit of diameter; the foot is the unit length. A wire of 1 mil diameter and 1 foot length is the unit size (mil-foot).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-20", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "The amount of resistance increase of a 1-ohm sample per degree rise in temperature above 0 degrees C is called the:", "options": ["Permeability", "Temperature coefficient of resistance", "Reluctance", "Resistivity"], "correct": 1, "short": "Temperature coefficient of resistance", "expl": "The temperature coefficient of resistance is the increase in resistance of a 1-ohm sample per degree rise above 0 degrees C. For copper it is approximately 0.00427 ohm.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-19", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "A device that converts light energy directly into electricity is a:", "options": ["Thermocouple", "Solar (photovoltaic) cell", "Piezo crystal", "Rectifier"], "correct": 1, "short": "Solar (photovoltaic) cell", "expl": "A solar cell or photovoltaic cell converts light energy into electricity; photons absorbed by semiconducting material (silicon) break electrons loose to produce current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-23", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "A thermocouple generates a voltage proportional to temperature at the junction of two dissimilar metals. This thermal-electric phenomenon is the:", "options": ["Edison effect", "Photovoltaic effect", "Seebeck effect", "Piezoelectric effect"], "correct": 2, "short": "Seebeck effect", "expl": "Thermocouples generate a voltage proportional to junction temperature — the Seebeck effect. They are used to measure cylinder head temperature and Turbine Inlet Temperature (TIT).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-23", "topic": "Sources of Electricity"}, {"q": "For most materials used as conductors, what happens to resistance as temperature rises?", "options": ["Resistance stays exactly constant", "Resistance drops to zero", "Resistance decreases", "Resistance increases"], "correct": 3, "short": "Resistance increases", "expl": "Most materials used as conductors increase in resistance as temperature increases. Carbon is an exception (it decreases); constantan and Manganin change very little.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-19", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "A change of TEN wire gauge numbers represents approximately what change in cross-sectional area?", "options": ["100:1", "10:1", "5:1", "2:1"], "correct": 1, "short": "10:1", "expl": "A change of ten wire gauge numbers represents a 10:1 change in cross-sectional area.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-20", "topic": "Conductors & Wire"}, {"q": "In electrical work, the prefix 'kilo' (k) means:", "options": ["One-thousandth", "One million", "One thousand", "One-millionth"], "correct": 2, "short": "One thousand", "expl": "Kilo (k) means one thousand. Mega (M) = one million; milli (m) = one-thousandth; micro (mu) = one-millionth.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Metric Prefixes"}, {"q": "The prefix 'micro' used in electrical calculations means:", "options": ["One million", "One thousand", "One-thousandth", "One-millionth"], "correct": 3, "short": "One-millionth", "expl": "Micro (mu) means one-millionth (1/1,000,000). Milli means one-thousandth. These appear constantly with farads (microfarad) and amps.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-4", "topic": "Metric Prefixes"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s4", "title": "Ohm's Law & Watt's Law (power)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> One law runs every circuit you will ever troubleshoot.\nIn water terms: <b>flow = pressure ÷ pinch</b>. In electrical terms:\n<b>current = voltage ÷ resistance</b>. That's Ohm's Law. Learn to spin it three\nways and the DC half of the FAA written is yours.</p>\n\n<h3>Ohm's Law, three ways</h3>\n<p>Said in English first: the current in a circuit equals the voltage pushing it\ndivided by the resistance fighting it. In symbols:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>I = E ÷ R</b> — current = volts over ohms (p. 12-17)</li>\n<li><b>E = I × R</b> — solve for voltage (p. 12-18)</li>\n<li><b>R = E ÷ I</b> — solve for resistance (p. 12-18)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The magic triangle:</b> draw E on top, I and R side-by-side on the bottom.\nCover the one you want; what's left showing is the math.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (the handbook's own)</span>\n<b>Find I:</b> 28 V across a 14-Ω load → I = 28 ÷ 14 = <b>2 A</b> (p. 12-18).<br>\n<b>Find I from the graph:</b> constant 20-Ω resistance, 60 V applied →\nI = 60 ÷ 20 = <b>3 A</b> (p. 12-17).<br>\n<b>Find E:</b> push 5 A through 4 Ω → E = 5 × 4 = <b>20 V</b> (p. 12-18).<br>\n<b>Find R:</b> 24 V produces 3 A → R = 24 ÷ 3 = <b>8 Ω</b> (p. 12-18).</div>\n\n<h3>Power — Watt's Law</h3>\n<p><b>Power is how FAST energy is being used.</b> Unit: the <b>watt</b>, which is\na rate of <b>1 joule per second</b> (p. 12-21). In water terms, power is\npressure × flow — how hard AND how much. Three interchangeable forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>P = I × E</b> (amps × volts)</li>\n<li><b>P = I&sup2; × R</b> (current squared × resistance)</li>\n<li><b>P = E&sup2; ÷ R</b> (voltage squared ÷ resistance)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (all p. 12-22)</span>\n<b>P = I × E:</b> 2 A at 24 V → <b>48 watts</b>.<br>\n<b>P = I&sup2;R:</b> 2 A through 100 Ω → 4 × 100 = <b>400 watts</b>.<br>\n<b>P = E&sup2;/R:</b> 24 V across 20 Ω → 576 ÷ 20 = <b>28.8 watts</b>.</div>\n<p>Two conversion facts the written loves: <b>1 horsepower = 746 watts</b>, and\none horsepower is defined as moving <b>33,000 pounds one foot in one minute</b>\n(p. 12-21).</p>\n<p>Two more power facts: in BOTH series and parallel circuits, <b>total power =\nthe sum of the power dissipated in each resistor</b> — power just adds, always\n(p. 12-22). And your electric bill's unit, the <b>kilowatt-hour</b>, is a unit\nof <b>energy</b> (power × time), not power (p. 12-22).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>I = E/R · E = I×R · R = E/I</b> (p. 12-17/12-18). Flow = pressure ÷ pinch.</li>\n<li><b>P = I×E = I&sup2;R = E&sup2;/R</b>; 1 watt = <b>1 joule/second</b> (p. 12-21/12-22).</li>\n<li><b>1 hp = 746 W = 33,000 ft-lb per minute</b> (p. 12-21).</li>\n<li>Total power = <b>sum of each resistor's power</b>, series OR parallel (p. 12-22).</li>\n<li>Kilowatt-hour = unit of <b>energy</b> (p. 12-22).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Ohm's Law, power, Watt's Law), pp. 12-17…12-22.</p>\n", "topics": ["Ohm's Law", "Power & Watt's Law"], "quiz": [{"q": "Ohm's Law states that current equals voltage divided by resistance. Which equation expresses this?", "options": ["I = E x R", "I = R / E", "I = E / R", "E = I / R"], "correct": 2, "short": "I = E / R", "expl": "Ohm's Law: I = E/R, where I is current (A), E is voltage (V), and R is resistance (ohms). Transposed: E = I x R and R = E/I.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "Using the third form of the power equation, P = E^2 / R, what is the power for 24 volts across 20 ohms?", "options": ["1.2 watts", "480 watts", "28.8 watts", "120 watts"], "correct": 2, "short": "28.8 watts", "expl": "P = E^2/R = (24 V)^2 / 20 ohms = 576 / 20 = 28.8 W. This matches the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-22", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Transposing Ohm's Law to solve for resistance gives which equation?", "options": ["R = E / I", "R = E + I", "R = I x E", "R = I / E"], "correct": 0, "short": "R = E / I", "expl": "Dividing E = I(R) by I gives the third form of Ohm's Law: R = E/I.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "One horsepower is defined as the power required to move how much weight one foot in one minute?", "options": ["1,000 pounds", "746 pounds", "33,000 pounds", "550 pounds"], "correct": 2, "short": "33,000 pounds", "expl": "One horsepower is required to move 33,000 pounds 1 foot in 1 minute, which works out to 550 ft-lb/s.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-21", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Transposing Ohm's Law to solve for voltage gives which equation?", "options": ["E = I / R", "E = I + R", "E = R / I", "E = I x R"], "correct": 3, "short": "E = I x R", "expl": "Multiplying I = E/R by R gives the second form of Ohm's Law: E = I(R).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "One horsepower of mechanical power is equivalent to how many watts of electrical power?", "options": ["33,000 watts", "1,000 watts", "746 watts", "550 watts"], "correct": 2, "short": "746 watts", "expl": "An electric motor rated at 1 horsepower requires 746 watts of electrical energy. (33,000 ft-lb/min and 550 ft-lb/s describe the mechanical definition of horsepower.)", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-21", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}, {"q": "Using the handbook's graph example with a constant 20-ohm resistance, if 60 volts are applied the current is:", "options": ["0.5 A", "3 A", "6 A", "30 A"], "correct": 1, "short": "3 amperes", "expl": "I = E/R = 60 V / 20 ohms = 3 A. This matches the handbook's linear-relationship graph (E=10 gives 0.5 A, E=60 gives 3 A, E=120 gives 6 A).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-17", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "If 24 volts produces 3 amperes of current, what is the resistance?", "options": ["27 ohms", "8 ohms", "72 ohms", "0.125 ohm"], "correct": 1, "short": "8 ohms", "expl": "R = E/I = 24 V / 3 A = 8 ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-18", "topic": "Ohm's Law"}, {"q": "A circuit carries 2 amps through a 100-ohm resistor. Using P = I^2 R, what is the power dissipated?", "options": ["400 watts", "50 watts", "10,000 watts", "200 watts"], "correct": 0, "short": "400 watts", "expl": "Second form of the power equation: P = I^2 R = (2 A)^2 x 100 ohms = 4 x 100 = 400 W. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-22", "topic": "Power & Watt's Law"}]}, {"id": "b4m1s5", "title": "Resistors & the color code", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A <b>resistor</b> is a deliberate pinch in the pipe — a\npart whose whole job is to <b>limit the amount of current flow</b> (p. 12-24).\nSmall ones are labeled with paint bands instead of printed numbers, so you have\nto read the color code.</p>\n\n<h3>How resistors are built</h3>\n<p>The everyday type is the <b>carbon composition</b> resistor: <b>the amount of\ngraphite relative to the insulation material determines its ohmic (resistive)\nvalue</b> — the graphite-to-insulation ratio; more graphite means more\nconductive paths and less resistance (p. 12-24). When a job needs to handle <b>large amounts of current with a\nhigh power rating</b>, you'll see <b>wire-wound</b> resistors instead — actual\nresistance wire wrapped on a ceramic core (p. 12-26).</p>\n\n<h3>The color code</h3>\n<p>Digits 0–9, in order: <b>Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4,\nGreen 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Grey 8, White 9</b> (p. 12-25).</p>\n<table><tr><th>Color</th><th>Digit</th><th>Color</th><th>Digit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Black</td><td>0</td><td>Green</td><td>5</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brown</td><td>1</td><td>Blue</td><td>6</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Red</b></td><td><b>2</b></td><td>Violet</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orange</td><td>3</td><td>Grey</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yellow</td><td>4</td><td>White</td><td>9</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Reading a 4-band (end-to-center) resistor:</b> band 1 = first digit,\nband 2 = second digit, band 3 = the <b>multiplier — the number of ZEROS to add</b>\nafter the first two digits (p. 12-25). Band 4 = tolerance.</p>\n<p><b>Tolerance</b> is the allowed percentage variation between the resistor's\nmarked value and its actual value (p. 12-24): <b>gold = ±5%, silver = ±10%,\nNO fourth band = ±20%</b> (p. 12-25).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples</span>\n<b>Red-Green-Yellow:</b> 2, 5, then four zeros → <b>250,000 ohms</b> (p. 12-25).<br>\n<b>Brown-Black-Red:</b> 1, 0, two zeros → 1,000 Ω = 1 kΩ. Gold 4th band → ±5%.</div>\n\n<h3>Variable resistors — an adjustable pinch</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rheostat:</b> a two-terminal variable resistor connected <b>in series</b>,\nused to <b>vary the CURRENT</b> in a circuit (p. 12-26). (Think dimmer valve.)</li>\n<li><b>Potentiometer:</b> a <b>THREE-terminal</b> device used to <b>vary the\nVOLTAGE</b> (p. 12-27). In a <b>linear</b> potentiometer, one quarter turn of the\nwiper gives <b>one quarter of the total resistance</b> — travel and resistance\ntrack evenly (p. 12-27).</li>\n<li><b>Thermistor:</b> a variable resistor with a <b>NEGATIVE temperature\ncoefficient</b> — as temperature increases, its resistance <b>decreases</b>\n(p. 12-27). (Backwards from plain wire on purpose — great for temp probes.)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Resistor's purpose: <b>limit current flow</b>; carbon composition value set by\n<b>graphite-to-insulation ratio</b>; wire-wound = <b>high current/power</b> (p. 12-24/12-26).</li>\n<li>Digits: Black 0 → White 9 (<b>Red = 2, Green = 5</b>); band 3 = <b>zeros to\nadd</b>; band 4 = tolerance (p. 12-25).</li>\n<li>Tolerance: <b>gold ±5%, silver ±10%, none ±20%</b>; tolerance = allowed %\nvariation from marked value (p. 12-24/12-25).</li>\n<li>Red-Green-Yellow = <b>250,000 Ω</b> (p. 12-25).</li>\n<li><b>Rheostat = series, varies current. Potentiometer = 3 terminals, varies\nvoltage</b> (linear pot: ¼ turn = ¼ resistance). Thermistor: temp ↑ → R ↓ (p. 12-26/12-27).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Resistors, color code, variable resistors), pp. 12-24…12-27.</p>\n", "topics": ["Resistors & Color Code"], "quiz": [{"q": "On a resistor, a GOLD fourth (tolerance) band indicates what tolerance?", "options": ["10 percent", "1 percent", "20 percent", "5 percent"], "correct": 3, "short": "5 percent", "expl": "A gold fourth band = 5 percent tolerance; silver = 10 percent; no fourth band = 20 percent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "The general purpose of a resistor in a circuit is to:", "options": ["Oppose AC only", "Increase voltage", "Store charge", "Limit the amount of current flow"], "correct": 3, "short": "Limit the amount of current flow", "expl": "The general use of a resistor is to limit the amount of current flow. Heat is typically the unwanted loss of energy (except in heating elements).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-24", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "A potentiometer is considered a THREE-terminal device and is used to vary the:", "options": ["Frequency", "Voltage in a circuit", "Current in a circuit", "Resistance of the source"], "correct": 1, "short": "Voltage in a circuit", "expl": "The potentiometer is a three-terminal device (terminal 3 is the wiper). While a rheostat varies current, the potentiometer is used to vary the voltage in a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-27", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "The percentage variation allowed between a resistor's marked value and its actual value is known as its:", "options": ["Wattage", "Tolerance", "Resistivity", "Reactance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Tolerance", "expl": "Tolerance is the allowable percentage variation between marked and actual value. A 5 percent resistor is not more than 5 percent higher or lower than the marked value.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-24", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "A resistor reads red, green, yellow. Its resistance value is:", "options": ["250,000 ohms", "25 ohms", "2,540 ohms", "2,500 ohms"], "correct": 0, "short": "250,000 ohms", "expl": "Red=2, Green=5, Yellow=4 (number of zeros). So 25 followed by four zeros = 250,000 ohms. With no fourth band, tolerance is 20 percent (50,000 ohms).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "Which variable resistor is used to VARY the current in a circuit and is connected in series?", "options": ["Rheostat", "Photocell", "Potentiometer", "Thermistor"], "correct": 0, "short": "Rheostat", "expl": "A rheostat is a variable resistor used to vary current; connected in series, increasing its resistance increases total resistance and decreases current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-26", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On the resistor color code, what numerical value does the color RED represent?", "options": ["2", "0", "5", "1"], "correct": 0, "short": "2", "expl": "Color code values: Black 0, Brown 1, Red 2, Orange 3, Yellow 4, Green 5, Blue 6, Violet 7, Gray 8, White 9.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On an end-to-center band resistor, the THIRD color band normally indicates:", "options": ["The first digit", "The second digit", "The number of zeros to add to the first two digits", "The wattage rating"], "correct": 2, "short": "Number of zeros to add", "expl": "The third band indicates the number of zeros to add to the two digits from the first and second bands (except gold = x 0.10, silver = x 0.01).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}, {"q": "On a resistor, a SILVER fourth (tolerance) band indicates what tolerance?", "options": ["20 percent", "1 percent", "5 percent", "10 percent"], "correct": 3, "short": "10 percent", "expl": "A silver fourth band = 10 percent tolerance. Gold = 5 percent; no band = 20 percent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-25", "topic": "Resistors & Color Code"}]}]}, {"n": 2, "day": "Unit 2", "title": "DC Circuits: Solving, Faults, Protection & Meters", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · DC circuits · ACS AM.I.A.K6, K7b, K8, K13, K14, K17-K19, K26", "sections": [{"id": "b4m2s1", "title": "Series, parallel & series-parallel circuits", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> There are only two ways to hook up loads — in a single\nline or side-by-side — and their rules are mirror images. Master the two rule\nsets, then combine them and you can solve any DC circuit on the written.</p>\n\n<h3>Series: one path</h3>\n<p>Components connected <b>end-to-end so the same current flows through each</b>,\nwith <b>only one path</b>, form a <b>series circuit</b> (p. 12-33). Beads on one\nstring. The rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Current is the SAME</b> through every component and at every point (p. 12-34).</li>\n<li><b>Total resistance = the SUM of the individual resistors</b> (p. 12-34).\nExample: 10 + 20 + 30 = <b>60 Ω</b> (p. 12-34).</li>\n<li>The voltage <b>divides</b> across the parts. The pressure loss forcing\nelectrons through each resistor is that resistor's <b>voltage drop</b> (p. 12-35).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (handbook's own)</span>\n<b>Current:</b> 12-V source, R1 = 10 Ω and R2 = 30 Ω in series →\nRT = 40 Ω → I = 12 ÷ 40 = <b>0.3 ampere</b> (p. 12-34).<br>\n<b>Source voltage:</b> 1 mA flows through 1 kΩ + 3 kΩ + 5 kΩ →\ndrops are 1 V + 3 V + 5 V → source = <b>9 volts</b> (p. 12-36).<br>\n<b>Cells in series:</b> three 1.5-V cells, same polarity → <b>4.5 V</b> total (p. 12-36).</div>\n\n<h3>Parallel: multiple paths</h3>\n<p>Side-by-side hookup. Each separate path is a <b>branch</b> (p. 12-39). The rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Voltage is the SAME across every branch</b> (p. 12-39).</li>\n<li><b>Total current = the SUM of the branch currents</b> (p. 12-39).</li>\n<li><b>Total resistance is ALWAYS LESS than the smallest branch resistor</b> —\nevery added pipe makes flowing easier (p. 12-39).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Finding parallel R:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Two resistors — product over sum:</b> RT = (R1 × R2) ÷ (R1 + R2)\n(p. 12-41). Example: 500 Ω and 400 Ω → 200,000 ÷ 900 ≈ <b>222.22 Ω</b> (p. 12-41).</li>\n<li><b>Equal resistors — divide by the count:</b> three 30-Ω resistors →\n30 ÷ 3 = <b>10 Ω</b> (p. 12-84).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A parallel circuit is also a <b>current divider</b>: the branches split the\ntotal current, and branches with <b>higher resistance carry LESS current</b> —\nflow takes the easier pipe (p. 12-41).</p>\n\n<h3>Series-parallel: the combination</h3>\n<p>A circuit mixing both hookups is a <b>series-parallel circuit</b> (p. 12-42).\nSolving recipe: <b>collapse the parallel clusters into single equivalent\nresistors, then add up the series chain.</b></p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">The handbook's worked example (p. 12-43)</span>\nR1 = 1 kΩ in series with [R2 = 2 kΩ parallel R3 = 3 kΩ], source ES = 24 V.<br>\nStep 1 — parallel cluster: (2k × 3k)÷(2k + 3k) = 6,000k ÷ 5k = 1.2 kΩ.<br>\nStep 2 — series add: RT = 1 kΩ + 1.2 kΩ = <b>2.2 kΩ</b>.<br>\nStep 3 — Ohm's Law: IT = 24 ÷ 2,200 ≈ <b>10.9 mA</b>.</div>\n<p><b>The classic failure difference:</b> one open in a series string kills the\nwhole circuit (old Christmas lights). One open branch in parallel — the other\nbranches keep running. That's why aircraft loads are wired in parallel.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series:</b> one path; current same; RT = sum; voltage divides as\n<b>voltage drops</b> (p. 12-33…12-36).</li>\n<li><b>Parallel:</b> voltage same across every <b>branch</b>; currents add;\n<b>RT &lt; smallest resistor</b> (p. 12-39).</li>\n<li>Two in parallel = <b>product over sum</b> (500 & 400 → 222.22 Ω); equal\nresistors = value ÷ count (three 30s → 10 Ω) (p. 12-41, 12-84).</li>\n<li>Current divider: higher-R branch → <b>less current</b> (p. 12-41).</li>\n<li>Series-parallel: collapse parallel first, then add. Handbook example:\nRT = <b>2.2 kΩ</b>, IT ≈ <b>10.9 mA</b> from 24 V (p. 12-42/12-43).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Series, parallel, series-parallel DC circuits), pp. 12-33…12-43.</p>\n", "topics": ["Series & Parallel Circuits"], "quiz": [{"q": "Three resistors of 10, 20, and 30 ohms are connected in SERIES. What is the total resistance?", "options": ["6 ohms", "20 ohms", "60 ohms", "30 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "60 ohms", "expl": "Series total resistance is the simple sum: 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-34", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a parallel circuit acting as a current divider, branches with higher resistance carry:", "options": ["More current", "Less current", "No current", "The same current as low-resistance branches"], "correct": 1, "short": "Less current", "expl": "Branch currents are inversely proportional to the ohmic values: branches with higher resistance have less current than those with lower resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a PARALLEL circuit, the total resistance is always:", "options": ["Equal to the largest branch", "The average of the branches", "Less than the value of the smallest resistor", "Equal to the sum of the branches"], "correct": 2, "short": "Less than the smallest branch", "expl": "The total resistance of a parallel combination is always less than the value of the smallest resistor, because adding branches gives current more paths.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-39", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "Using the product-over-sum formula, a 500-ohm and 400-ohm resistor in parallel give a total resistance of approximately:", "options": ["900 ohms", "450 ohms", "100 ohms", "222.22 ohms"], "correct": 3, "short": "222.22 ohms", "expl": "RT = (500 x 400) / (500 + 400) = 200,000 / 900 = 222.22 ohms. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "A circuit that is a combination of resistors in series and in parallel is called a:", "options": ["Series-parallel circuit", "Tank circuit", "Resonant circuit", "Bridge circuit"], "correct": 0, "short": "Series-parallel circuit", "expl": "Most real circuits are series-parallel — combinations of resistors in parallel and in series; the same series and parallel rules apply.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-42", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In the handbook example, a current of 1 mA flows through R1=1k, R2=3k, R3=5k in series. The source voltage equals:", "options": ["3 volts", "1 volt", "5 volts", "9 volts"], "correct": 3, "short": "9 volts", "expl": "Voltage drops: 1 V + 3 V + 5 V = 9 V. Or ES = I x RT = 1 mA x 9 k = 9 V. The sum of the drops equals the source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In the handbook's series-parallel example (R1 = 1k in series with R2=2k parallel R3=3k, ES = 24 V), the total resistance RT is:", "options": ["6 k-ohm", "5 k-ohm", "1.2 k-ohm", "2.2 k-ohm"], "correct": 3, "short": "2.2 k-ohms", "expl": "R23 = (2k x 3k)/(2k+3k) = 6,000k/5k = 1.2k. RT = R1 + R23 = 1k + 1.2k = 2.2k ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-43", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In that same series-parallel example (RT = 2.2 k-ohm, ES = 24 V), the total current IT is approximately:", "options": ["10.9 mA", "24 mA", "52.8 mA", "2.2 mA"], "correct": 0, "short": "10.9 mA", "expl": "IT = ES/RT = 24 V / 2.2k = 10.9 mA (handbook worked example).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-43", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}, {"q": "In a SERIES circuit, the total resistance equals:", "options": ["The sum of the individual resistors", "Less than the smallest resistor", "The product of the resistors", "The smallest resistor value"], "correct": 0, "short": "The sum of the resistors", "expl": "For resistors in series, total resistance = R1 + R2 + R3 + ... + RN (a simple sum).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-34", "topic": "Series & Parallel Circuits"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s2", "title": "Kirchhoff's laws & voltage dividers", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Kirchhoff's two laws are the bookkeeping of electricity:\n<b>nothing gets created, nothing gets lost</b> — not flow at a junction, not\npressure around a loop. Every drop of water that enters a tee leaves it; every\npsi the pump makes gets spent somewhere in the loop.</p>\n\n<h3>Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)</h3>\n<p>Around any <b>closed loop</b>, the <b>algebraic sum of ALL voltages\n(source and drops) is ZERO</b> (p. 12-36). Stated the friendlier way: <b>the\nsource voltage equals the SUM of the voltage drops</b> (p. 12-36). Same law,\ntwo phrasings — the written uses both.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (p. 12-36)</span>\n<b>Find the source:</b> series drops of 14 V and 10 V → source = 14 + 10 =\n<b>24 volts</b>.<br>\n<b>Find a missing drop:</b> 24-V source, three series resistors, two drops are\n12 V and 10 V → E3 = 24 − 12 − 10 = <b>2 volts</b>.</div>\n\n<h3>Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)</h3>\n<p>At any <b>junction (node)</b>: <b>the sum of currents entering equals the sum\nof currents leaving</b> (p. 12-41). Electrons don't pile up in the fitting.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (p. 12-41)</span>\n<b>Currents in:</b> I1 = 5 mA and I2 = 12 mA enter a node → total leaving =\n5 + 12 = <b>17 mA</b>.<br>\n<b>Missing branch:</b> 75 mA enters; two branches carry 30 mA and 20 mA →\nthird branch = 75 − 30 − 20 = <b>25 mA</b>.</div>\n\n<h3>Voltage dividers — KVL put to work</h3>\n<p>A <b>voltage divider</b> is a device that makes it possible to <b>obtain more\nthan one voltage from a single power source</b> — usually a string of resistors\nin series with fixed or movable taps (p. 12-37). Each resistor's share of the\nsource is proportional to its share of the resistance.</p>\n<p>The formula, in English: <i>a resistor's drop is its fraction of the total\nresistance times the source.</i> In symbols: <b>E<sub>X</sub> =\n(R<sub>X</sub> ÷ R<sub>T</sub>) × E<sub>S</sub></b>.</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example (p. 12-39)</span>\nRT = 1,000 Ω, source ES = 100 V. Drop across a 300-Ω resistor:\n(300 ÷ 1,000) × 100 = <b>30 V</b>.</div>\n<p>One vocabulary term rides along: in a divider, a small current flows through\nthe resistor string itself in addition to the load currents. That's the\n<b>bleeder current</b> — it stabilizes the divider's output voltages (p. 12-37).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>KVL:</b> around a closed loop the algebraic sum of all voltages =\n<b>ZERO</b>; equivalently, source = <b>sum of the drops</b> (p. 12-36).</li>\n<li><b>KCL:</b> at any junction, current entering = current leaving (p. 12-41).</li>\n<li>Voltage divider = <b>multiple voltages from one source</b>;\n<b>E<sub>X</sub> = (R<sub>X</sub>/R<sub>T</sub>) × E<sub>S</sub></b> →\n300/1,000 × 100 V = <b>30 V</b> (p. 12-37/12-39).</li>\n<li>The divider's own housekeeping current = <b>bleeder current</b> (p. 12-37).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Kirchhoff's laws, voltage dividers), pp. 12-36…12-41.</p>\n", "topics": ["Kirchhoff's Laws", "Voltage Dividers"], "quiz": [{"q": "Using Kirchhoff's Current Law: if branch currents I1 = 5 mA and I2 = 12 mA enter a node, the total current is:", "options": ["7 mA", "12 mA", "60 mA", "17 mA"], "correct": 3, "short": "17 mA", "expl": "IT = I1 + I2 = 5 mA + 12 mA = 17 mA. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "Using the voltage divider formula EX = (RX/RT) x ES with RT = 1,000 ohms, ES = 100 V, the drop across a 300-ohm resistor is:", "options": ["30 V", "100 V", "10 V", "60 V"], "correct": 0, "short": "30 volts", "expl": "EX = (300/1000) x 100 = 30 V. The voltage drop across a resistor equals the ratio of its resistance to total resistance, times the source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-39", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Kirchhoff's Current Law states that at any junction (node):", "options": ["Current leaving exceeds current entering", "Current is the same in every branch", "Voltage drops sum to zero", "The sum of currents entering equals the sum of currents leaving"], "correct": 3, "short": "Currents in = currents out", "expl": "Kirchhoff's Current Law: the sum of the currents into a junction equals the sum of the currents flowing out of that same junction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "A 24-volt source feeds three series resistors. Two drops are 12 V and 10 V. Using KVL, the third drop (E3) is:", "options": ["46 volts", "2 volts", "12 volts", "22 volts"], "correct": 1, "short": "2 volts", "expl": "24 - 12 - 10 - E3 = 0, so E3 = 2 volts. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "In a voltage divider, the small current that flows through the divider in addition to the load currents is called the:", "options": ["Branch current", "Bleeder current", "Eddy current", "Drift current"], "correct": 1, "short": "Bleeder current", "expl": "In addition to the load currents (I1, I2, I3), some bleeder current (IB) flows. The total current IT is the sum of all currents.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-37", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Total current into a node is 75 mA. Two of three branches carry 30 mA and 20 mA. The third branch carries:", "options": ["125 mA", "50 mA", "15 mA", "25 mA"], "correct": 3, "short": "25 mA", "expl": "IT = I1 + I2 + I3, so 75 = 30 + I2 + 20, giving I2 = 25 mA. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-41", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "A device that makes it possible to obtain more than one voltage from a single power source — usually resistors connected in series with fixed/movable contacts — is a:", "options": ["Voltage divider", "Rectifier", "Transformer", "Current divider"], "correct": 0, "short": "Voltage divider", "expl": "Voltage dividers make it possible to obtain more than one voltage from a single power source; they consist of resistors in series with tap contacts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-37", "topic": "Voltage Dividers"}, {"q": "Stated another way, Kirchhoff's Voltage Law tells us the source voltage equals:", "options": ["The sum of the voltage drops", "Zero", "The total current", "The largest single voltage drop"], "correct": 0, "short": "Sum of the voltage drops", "expl": "The source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops: ES = E1 + E2 + E3 + ... + EN.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}, {"q": "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law states that around any closed loop, the algebraic sum of all voltages is:", "options": ["Equal to the current", "Equal to the largest drop", "Zero", "Equal to total resistance"], "correct": 2, "short": "Zero", "expl": "Kirchhoff's Voltage Law: the algebraic sum of all voltages around a closed loop is zero. Equivalently, the sum of all voltage drops equals the total source voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-36", "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s3", "title": "Opens, shorts & continuity troubleshooting", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Circuits fail two opposite ways: the pipe gets <b>cut</b>\n(open — no flow) or it <b>bursts a shortcut</b> (short — way too much flow).\nYour meters find which one you have. This is pure troubleshooting — the skill\nthe ACS tests hands-on (AM.I.A.S1: perform a continuity test).</p>\n\n<h3>The two faults</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An <b>OPEN circuit</b> is a circuit that is <b>not a complete or continuous\npath</b> — electrically it looks like an <b>infinitely large resistance</b>, so\n<b>no current flows</b> (p. 12-79). Cut wire, blown fuse, burned-out filament.</li>\n<li>A <b>SHORT circuit</b> is an <b>unintentional low-resistance path</b> between\ntwo points or to ground, usually creating <b>high current flow</b> (p. 12-78).\nChafed insulation lets the wire touch structure; resistance collapses; current\nskyrockets — heat, smoke, popped breakers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Continuity</b> is the healthy state: continuous, uninterrupted, connected\ntogether — a circuit that is not broken (p. 12-79). A continuity test simply\nasks the ohmmeter: \"is this path unbroken?\"</p>\n\n<h3>Finding an OPEN with a voltmeter (series circuits)</h3>\n<p>In a series circuit, all the current stops at the break — and so does all\nthe voltage. Probe across each component: the good ones read ~0 V, and <b>the\nopen component shows the TOTAL (source) voltage across it</b> (p. 12-82). Full\npressure sits waiting at the closed valve.</p>\n\n<h3>Why the voltmeter lies in PARALLEL circuits</h3>\n<p>Checking for an open branch in a <b>parallel</b> circuit with a voltmeter\ndoesn't work: <b>across the open resistor you still read the voltage drop of\nthe parallel branch beside it</b> — the healthy branches hold the voltage up,\nmasking the fault (p. 12-84). Use an ohmmeter on the isolated branch instead.</p>\n\n<h3>Ohmmeter rules & the shorted-resistor symptom</h3>\n<p>To accurately check a component's resistance, it must first be\n<b>isolated/disconnected and the power removed</b> (p. 12-82) — otherwise\nparallel paths (or live voltage) corrupt the reading.</p>\n<p>And the short-circuit signature in a series string: a <b>shorted resistor\nmakes the circuit current INCREASE — greater than normal current flow</b> —\nbecause you deleted part of the pinch, so more flows than the circuit was\ndesigned for (usually popping the fuse) (p. 12-83).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open</b> = not continuous, <b>infinite resistance, no current</b> (p. 12-79).\n<b>Short</b> = unintentional <b>low-resistance path, high current</b> (p. 12-78).</li>\n<li><b>Continuity</b> = continuous/unbroken path (p. 12-79).</li>\n<li>Series open: the open component reads <b>full source voltage</b> on a voltmeter\n(p. 12-82).</li>\n<li>Parallel open: voltmeter <b>can't</b> find it (reads the branch drop);\nisolate and use the ohmmeter (p. 12-84).</li>\n<li>Ohmmeter checks: component <b>isolated + power removed</b> (p. 12-82).</li>\n<li>Shorted resistor in series → current <b>increases</b> (p. 12-83).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Open/short circuits, continuity, DC troubleshooting), pp. 12-78…12-84.</p>\n", "topics": ["Open & Short Circuits"], "quiz": [{"q": "A circuit that is not a complete or continuous path — representing an infinitely large resistance so no current flows — is a(n):", "options": ["Parallel circuit", "Short circuit", "Closed circuit", "Open circuit"], "correct": 3, "short": "Open circuit", "expl": "An open circuit is not a complete or continuous path; it represents an infinitely large resistance. Switches and burned-out components are common causes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-79", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "The state of being continuous, uninterrupted, or connected together (a circuit that is not broken) is called:", "options": ["Reactance", "Discontinuity", "Saturation", "Continuity"], "correct": 3, "short": "Continuity", "expl": "Continuity is the state of being continuous, uninterrupted, or connected together — the opposite of a broken or open circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-79", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "When checking for an open in a PARALLEL circuit, why can a voltmeter NOT be used effectively?", "options": ["It cannot measure AC", "Across an open resistor it reads the voltage drop of the parallel branch", "It draws too much current", "It has too high resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Reads parallel branch voltage", "expl": "A voltmeter cannot be used in a parallel circuit for an open, since placed across an open resistor it reads the voltage drop in a parallel branch. Use an ammeter or modified ohmmeter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-84", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "When tracing an OPEN in a SERIES circuit with a voltmeter, the open component will show:", "options": ["Half the source voltage", "Zero volts", "The total (source) voltage across it", "A negative voltage"], "correct": 2, "short": "Total source voltage", "expl": "With an open in a series circuit, no current flows so good components drop 0 V; the total source voltage appears across the open component (per Kirchhoff's Voltage Law).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-82", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "In a SERIES circuit, a shorted resistor causes the circuit current to:", "options": ["Decrease", "Increase (greater than normal current flow)", "Drop to zero", "Stay the same"], "correct": 1, "short": "Increase", "expl": "A short across a series circuit produces a greater-than-normal current flow; usually this blows the fuse or burns out a component.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-83", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "An unintentional low-resistance path between two points (or to ground) that usually creates high current flow is called a:", "options": ["Series circuit", "Short circuit", "Voltage divider", "Open circuit"], "correct": 1, "short": "Short circuit", "expl": "A short circuit is an unintentional low-resistance path; it usually creates high current flow that burns out or damages the conductor or components.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-78", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}, {"q": "To accurately check the resistance of a component with an ohmmeter, the component must first be:", "options": ["Shorted", "Heated", "Energized", "Isolated/disconnected and the power removed"], "correct": 3, "short": "Isolated and power removed", "expl": "When using an ohmmeter, the component must be isolated and the power source removed, otherwise parallel paths cause inaccurate readings or meter damage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-82", "topic": "Open & Short Circuits"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s4", "title": "Circuit protection, switches & relays", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> When a short happens, something has to give — and it must\nNOT be the wiring harness. Protection devices are the sacrificial weak links,\nand switches/relays are the valves that control the flow on purpose.</p>\n\n<h3>The fault they protect against</h3>\n<p>A <b>direct short</b> is when a point at full system voltage comes into\ndirect contact with the ground/return side — a path with <b>virtually no\nresistance</b> (p. 12-27). Ohm's Law with a near-zero R means enormous current:\nwires glow, insulation burns. Protection devices exist to open the circuit\nbefore that happens — they protect the <b>wire</b>, not the radio.</p>\n\n<h3>The protection lineup</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fuse:</b> a metal strip that melts open. The fuse sits in the circuit so\nALL the circuit's current passes through it; in most fuses the strip is an\nalloy of <b>tin and bismuth</b>, chosen because it <b>melts and opens the\ncircuit</b> the moment current exceeds the fuse's rated capacity (p. 12-28).\nMelted = replace it.</li>\n<li><b>Current limiter:</b> similar idea but made of <b>copper</b>, used in\n<b>heavy-current circuits (30 A or greater)</b>; it takes a sustained overload\nbefore it opens, and once opened it <b>must be replaced</b> (p. 12-29).</li>\n<li><b>Circuit breaker:</b> interrupts on overload and <b>CAN be reset</b> —\nthat's its whole advantage over the fuse (p. 12-29). Aircraft rule you must\nknow: the <b>AUTOMATIC RESET breaker is NOT allowed in aircraft</b> — a breaker\nthat keeps reconnecting a faulted circuit by itself is a fire machine\n(p. 12-29).</li>\n<li><b>Thermal protector (thermal switch):</b> protects a <b>MOTOR</b>; it opens\nthe circuit when the motor <b>becomes excessively hot</b>, then re-closes when\nit cools (p. 12-29).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Switches — poles, throws, positions</h3>\n<p>Switch names are just counts (p. 12-30):\n<b>POLES = the number of circuits (paths) the switch can complete at one time</b>;\n<b>THROWS = the number of circuits each pole can complete</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SPST</b> — single-pole single-throw: one connection between two contacts,\nopen in one position, closed in the other (p. 12-30).</li>\n<li><b>DPDT</b> — double-pole double-throw: makes a connection <b>from one set of\ncontacts to either of two other sets</b> (p. 12-30).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A switch that <b>stays open except while held closed</b> is <b>normally open\n(NO)</b>; one that sits closed until pushed is normally closed (NC) (p. 12-31).</p>\n<p>The <b>microswitch</b> opens or closes with a tiny movement of its plunger —\n<b>1/16 inch or less</b> — which is why it's the classic <b>limit switch for\nlanding gear</b> position sensing (p. 12-31).</p>\n\n<h3>Relays — a switch thrown by electricity</h3>\n<p>A <b>relay</b> is an electromechanical switch in which a <b>SMALL current\nenergizes a coil to control a LARGE current</b> (p. 12-33). The cockpit switch\ncarries milliamps; the relay contacts carry the starter's hundreds of amps.</p>\n<p>Contact logic: when the coil energizes and pulls the arm down, the contacts\nbetween the arm and the <b>normally closed (NC)</b> contacts are <b>OPENED</b>\n(and the normally-open side closes) — energizing flips every contact to its\nopposite state (p. 12-33).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Direct short = full voltage to ground, <b>virtually no resistance</b> (p. 12-27).</li>\n<li>Fuse strip = <b>tin & bismuth</b>; <b>current limiter = copper, 30 A+,\nreplace once opened</b>; breaker = resettable (p. 12-28/12-29).</li>\n<li><b>Automatic-reset breakers: NOT allowed in aircraft</b> (p. 12-29).</li>\n<li>Thermal protector opens when the <b>motor overheats</b> (p. 12-29).</li>\n<li><b>Poles</b> = circuits completed at once; <b>throws</b> = circuits per pole;\nSPST / DPDT; <b>NO = open unless held closed</b>; microswitch = 1/16-inch travel,\nlanding-gear limit switch (p. 12-30/12-31).</li>\n<li>Relay: <b>small coil current switches a big load</b>; energizing OPENS the\nNC contacts (p. 12-33).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Circuit protection, switches, relays), pp. 12-27…12-33.</p>\n", "topics": ["Circuit Protection", "Switches & Relays"], "quiz": [{"q": "In most fuses, the metal strip that melts to open the circuit is made of an alloy of:", "options": ["Copper and aluminum", "Iron and nickel", "Lead and silver", "Tin and bismuth"], "correct": 3, "short": "Tin and bismuth", "expl": "In most fuses, the metal strip is made of an alloy of tin and bismuth, which melts and opens the circuit when current exceeds the rated capacity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-28", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "A switch that stays open except when held in the closed position is identified as:", "options": ["Locking", "Normally closed (NC)", "Double throw", "Normally open (NO)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Normally open (NO)", "expl": "A switch that stays open except when held in the closed position is a normally open (NO) switch. One that stays closed except when held open is normally closed (NC).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-31", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "A switch that allows a connection between two contacts — either open in one position or closed in the other — is designated:", "options": ["DPDT", "SPDT", "DPST", "SPST"], "correct": 3, "short": "SPST (single-pole, single-throw)", "expl": "The single-pole, single-throw (SPST) switch allows a connection between two contacts; the circuit is open in one position and closed in the other.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-30", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "A circuit-protection device similar to a fuse but made of copper, used in heavy-current circuits (30 amp or greater) and which must be replaced once opened, is a:", "options": ["Current limiter", "Relay", "Circuit breaker", "Thermal switch"], "correct": 0, "short": "Current limiter", "expl": "The current limiter's link is usually copper and stands a considerable overload briefly; it opens in over-current heavy circuits (30 A+), is used to sectionalize a bus, and must be replaced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-29", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "A switch that makes a connection from one set of contacts to either of two other sets of contacts is designated:", "options": ["SPST", "SPDT", "DPST", "DPDT"], "correct": 3, "short": "DPDT (double-pole, double-throw)", "expl": "The double-pole, double-throw (DPDT) switch makes a connection from one set of contacts to either of two other sets of contacts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-30", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "Which type of circuit breaker is NOT allowed in aircraft?", "options": ["Magnetic type", "Automatic reset type", "Thermal overload type", "Manually reset type"], "correct": 1, "short": "Automatic reset type", "expl": "Automatic reset type circuit breakers are not allowed in aircraft. Most circuit breakers must be reset by hand.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-29", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "A thermal protector (thermal switch) used to protect a MOTOR works by opening the circuit when the motor:", "options": ["Becomes excessively hot", "Draws too little current", "Spins too slowly", "Loses voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Becomes excessively hot", "expl": "A thermal protector opens the circuit automatically whenever the motor becomes excessively hot, using a bimetallic disk/strip that bends when heated.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-29", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}, {"q": "On a relay, when the coil is energized and the arm is pulled down, the contacts between the arm and the normally closed (NC) contacts are:", "options": ["Shorted", "Closed", "Unchanged", "Opened"], "correct": 3, "short": "Opened", "expl": "When energized, the electromagnetic field pulls the arm down: the circuit between the arm and the NC contacts opens, and between the arm and the NO contacts closes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-33", "topic": "Switches & Relays"}, {"q": "A situation in which a point at full system voltage comes into direct contact with the ground/return side, creating a path with virtually no resistance, is called a:", "options": ["Open circuit", "Voltage drop", "Direct short", "Series fault"], "correct": 2, "short": "Direct short", "expl": "A direct short describes full system voltage coming into direct contact with ground, establishing a path with virtually no resistance and causing rapid heat generation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-27", "topic": "Circuit Protection"}]}, {"id": "b4m2s5", "title": "Measuring instruments (volt/amp/ohm/megger)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Meters are your pressure gauge and flow gauge. Every\nanalog meter is built around one little machine, and every measurement question\ncomes down to: <i>how is it connected, and what's its internal resistance?</i></p>\n\n<h3>The heart: the d'Arsonval movement</h3>\n<p>The basic DC meter movement — inside the ammeter, voltmeter, AND ohmmeter —\nis the <b>d'Arsonval movement</b>: current through a small coil in a magnetic\nfield deflects a pointer in proportion to the current (p. 12-68). The current\nneeded to swing it to <b>full-scale deflection</b> is the movement's <b>current\nsensitivity</b> (p. 12-68).</p>\n\n<h3>Ammeter — the flow gauge</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Connected <b>IN SERIES</b> with the element being measured — all the flow\nmust pass through the gauge (p. 12-69).</li>\n<li>To read currents bigger than the movement can take, a <b>SHUNT resistor</b>\nis added in parallel with the movement to <b>bypass the excess current</b>\n(p. 12-69).</li>\n<li><b>NEVER connect an ammeter across a voltage source:</b> its very low\nresistance allows excessive current to flow and damages it (p. 12-70).</li>\n<li>Most accurate readings come at about <b>half-scale deflection</b> — plan\nyour range so the needle lands mid-scale (p. 12-70).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Voltmeter — the pressure gauge</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Connected <b>IN PARALLEL</b> — across the two points whose pressure\ndifference you want (p. 12-71).</li>\n<li>To extend its range, a <b>MULTIPLIER resistor</b> goes in series with the\nmovement to drop the excess voltage (p. 12-70).</li>\n<li>Voltmeter sensitivity is rated in <b>ohms per volt</b> (resistance per volt)\n(p. 12-70). Use a <b>high-resistance</b> voltmeter so the meter itself doesn't\n<b>shunt</b> current away and change the reading (p. 12-71).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Memory hook</span>\n<b>A</b>mmeter: <b>A</b> = in-line (series), low resistance, shunt extends range.<br>\n<b>V</b>oltmeter: <b>V</b> = across (parallel), high resistance, multiplier extends range.</div>\n\n<h3>Ohmmeter & megger</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The ohmmeter measures resistance and — unlike the other two — contains its\nown <b>battery</b> (p. 12-71). Power OFF the circuit first.</li>\n<li>Leads apart (open): pointer shows <b>infinite resistance</b>. The scale reads\nbackwards, decreasing left to right — the <b>back-off scale</b> (p. 12-71).</li>\n<li>The <b>MEGGER (megohmmeter)</b> is a high-range ohmmeter with a\n<b>hand-operated generator</b>, used to measure <b>insulation resistance</b>.\nIts chief advantage: it tests with a high potential — a <b>breakdown\nvoltage</b> — that finds insulation faults a low-voltage ohmmeter would miss\n(p. 12-72).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Measuring AC, power, and waveforms</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A plain d'Arsonval meter on AC reads <b>ZERO</b> — it averages the cycle, and\na sine wave's average is zero (p. 12-73). To measure AC it needs a\n<b>rectifier</b> that converts the AC to DC first (p. 12-73).</li>\n<li>The <b>moving iron vane meter</b> — two concentric iron vanes (one fixed, one\nmovable) inside a solenoid, working by repulsion — measures <b>either AC or\nDC</b> (p. 12-74).</li>\n<li>Power is measured with a <b>wattmeter</b>. Because power is the PRODUCT of\ncurrent and voltage (P = I × E), a wattmeter must have <b>two elements — one for\ncurrent and one for voltage</b> — so it is usually of the\n<b>electrodynamometer</b> type: its deflection is proportional to volts ×\namps, which IS watts (p. 12-76).</li>\n<li>To SEE a waveform (phase, amplitude, duration) you use the\n<b>oscilloscope</b>, classically built on a cathode-ray tube (p. 12-76).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Multimeters</h3>\n<p>The combined analog volt + ohm + milliammeter is the multimeter or <b>VOM\n(volt-ohm-milliammeter)</b> (p. 12-80). Modern <b>digital multimeters (DMM/DVM)</b>\nbeat analog meters with greater accuracy and <b>higher input resistance</b> —\nless loading effect on the circuit being measured (p. 12-80).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>d'Arsonval</b> = the basic DC movement; full-scale current = <b>current\nsensitivity</b> (p. 12-68).</li>\n<li>Ammeter: <b>series</b>, low R, <b>shunt</b> extends range, never across a\nsource, best at <b>half-scale</b> (p. 12-69/12-70).</li>\n<li>Voltmeter: <b>parallel</b>, high R (avoid shunting), <b>multiplier</b> extends\nrange, sensitivity in <b>ohms/volt</b> (p. 12-70/12-71).</li>\n<li>Ohmmeter: own <b>battery</b>, open leads = <b>infinity</b>, back-off scale;\n<b>megger</b> = hand-generator ohmmeter for insulation, tests at breakdown\nvoltage (p. 12-71/12-72).</li>\n<li>d'Arsonval on AC reads <b>zero</b> (needs a rectifier); iron-vane meter reads\nAC or DC; wattmeter = <b>electrodynamometer</b>; waveform viewer =\n<b>oscilloscope</b> (p. 12-73…12-76).</li>\n<li>VOM = analog multimeter; DMM = accuracy + <b>higher input resistance</b> (p. 12-80).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Measuring instruments), pp. 12-68…12-80.</p>\n", "topics": ["Measuring Instruments"], "quiz": [{"q": "On an ohmmeter, when the leads are OPEN (not connected), the pointer indicates:", "options": ["Half-scale", "A short circuit", "Infinite resistance", "Zero ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "Infinite resistance", "expl": "When the ohmmeter leads are open, the meter indicates infinite resistance (an open circuit). When the leads are shorted, it reads zero.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-71", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "A high-range ohmmeter containing a hand-operated generator, used to measure insulation resistance with a high 'breakdown' voltage, is a:", "options": ["Wheatstone bridge", "Megger (megohmmeter)", "Ammeter", "Multimeter"], "correct": 1, "short": "Megger (megohmmeter)", "expl": "The megger (megohmmeter) is a high-range ohmmeter with a hand-operated generator, used to measure insulation resistance, ground, continuity, and short circuits at high potential.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-72", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The most accurate ammeter readings are obtained at approximately what point on the scale?", "options": ["Quarter-scale deflection", "Zero deflection", "Full-scale deflection", "Half-scale deflection"], "correct": 3, "short": "Half-scale deflection", "expl": "The most accurate readings are obtained at approximately half-scale deflection; start with the highest range and work down.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-70", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Compared with older analog meters, digital multimeters (DMM) and digital voltmeters (DVM) offer greater accuracy and:", "options": ["Slower readings", "Higher input resistance (less loading effect)", "Lower input resistance (more loading)", "No need for a power source"], "correct": 1, "short": "Higher input resistance", "expl": "The DVM/DMM offers greater accuracy, less ambiguity, and higher input resistance, which gives it less of a loading effect on a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-80", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Voltmeter sensitivity is defined in terms of:", "options": ["Resistance per volt (ohms/volt)", "Volts per ampere", "Amps per ohm", "Watts per volt"], "correct": 0, "short": "Resistance per volt (ohms/volt)", "expl": "Voltmeter sensitivity is defined in terms of resistance per volt (ohms/volt). A 50 microamp movement has a sensitivity of 20k ohms/volt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-70", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "Electric power is measured by means of a wattmeter, which has two elements (one for current, one for voltage) and is usually of which type?", "options": ["d'Arsonval type", "Electrodynamometer type", "Inclined coil type", "Moving iron vane type"], "correct": 1, "short": "Electrodynamometer type", "expl": "Because power is the product of current and voltage, a wattmeter needs two elements and is usually of the electrodynamometer type; it can measure AC or DC power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-76", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The amount of current required to drive a meter movement to full-scale deflection is called the meter's:", "options": ["Current sensitivity", "Multiplier", "Resistance", "Damping"], "correct": 0, "short": "Current sensitivity", "expl": "Current sensitivity is the amount of current required to drive the movement to full-scale deflection (e.g., a 1 mA movement needs 1 mA for full scale).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-68", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The basic DC meter movement (used in the ammeter, voltmeter, and ohmmeter) that deflects a pointer in proportion to current through a coil is the:", "options": ["Electrodynamometer movement", "Moving iron vane movement", "d'Arsonval movement", "Inclined coil movement"], "correct": 2, "short": "d'Arsonval movement", "expl": "The d'Arsonval meter movement is the basic DC current-measuring device used in the ammeter, voltmeter, and ohmmeter; the pointer deflects in proportion to coil current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-68", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}, {"q": "The combined analog instrument that includes the voltmeter, ammeter, and ohmmeter is commonly called a multimeter or:", "options": ["Megger", "CRT", "DIP", "VOM (volt-ohm-milliammeter)"], "correct": 3, "short": "VOM", "expl": "The combined voltmeter/ammeter/ohmmeter is called a multimeter or a VOM (volt-ohm-milliammeter). Digital versions are the DMM and DVM.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-80", "topic": "Measuring Instruments"}]}]}, {"n": 3, "day": "Unit 3", "title": "Magnetism, Batteries & AC Fundamentals", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Magnetism, cells, AC · ACS AM.I.A.K2, K5, K7d/e, K15", "sections": [{"id": "b4m3s1", "title": "Magnetism", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Magnetism is the bridge between mechanical motion and\nelectricity — generators, motors, relays, and transformers all live on this\nbridge. First, the bar-magnet basics and the vocabulary the written drills.</p>\n\n<h3>What's magnetic — and what isn't</h3>\n<p><b>Magnetism</b> is the property of attracting certain metallic substances —\ngenerally iron and iron alloys, called <b>ferrous materials</b> (p. 12-6).\nThree <b>nonferrous</b> materials are magnetic to a limited degree:\n<b>nickel, cobalt, and gadolinium</b> (p. 12-6).</p>\n<p>The three classes: iron and its alloys, with by far the greatest\npermeability, are <b>ferromagnetic</b> (p. 12-9); weakly-attracted materials are\nparamagnetic; and substances actually <b>REPELLED by both poles</b> are\n<b>diamagnetic</b> (p. 12-6).</p>\n\n<h3>Poles and flux</h3>\n<p>Hang a bar magnet on a string and one end swings toward Earth's north — that\nend is precisely the <b>north-SEEKING pole</b> (p. 12-6). Like poles repel,\nunlike poles attract — same law as static charge.</p>\n<p>The field is drawn as lines of force, also called <b>magnetic flux</b>\n(p. 12-8), and flux lines have personalities (p. 12-8):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They <b>never cross one another — they actually repel each other</b>.</li>\n<li>Outside the magnet they run N → S and form closed loops.</li>\n<li>When a piece of magnetic material can accept no more lines of force, it is\n<b>SATURATED</b> (p. 12-8).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Permeability & reluctance — the magnetic pipe words</h3>\n<p><b>Permeability</b> = how EASILY flux can penetrate a material (magnetic\nconductivity) — the scale is based on a <b>perfect vacuum, rated 1</b>\n(p. 12-9). <b>Reluctance</b> = a material's OPPOSITION to lines of force —\ndirectly comparable to <b>resistance</b> in an electrical circuit (p. 12-9).\nPressure/flow/pinch, magnetic edition.</p>\n\n<h3>Keeping and losing magnetism</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A magnet that keeps its magnetism long after the magnetizing force is gone =\na <b>permanent magnet</b> (p. 12-9); the ability to hold magnetism is\n<b>retentivity</b> (p. 12-11).</li>\n<li>The small amount left behind in soft iron after the force is removed =\n<b>residual magnetism</b> — critically important to generators, which need that\nleftover field to build up output on startup (p. 12-11).</li>\n<li><b>Heating, hammering, or dropping</b> a magnet makes its strength\n<b>DECREASE</b> — you're knocking the aligned domains out of line (p. 12-8).</li>\n<li>Storage: install <b>keeper bars</b> across the poles to <b>provide an easy\nflux path and retain (promote retention of) the magnetism</b> (p. 12-8).</li>\n<li>The champion permanent-magnet material: <b>Alnico</b> — an alloy of iron,\n<b>al</b>uminum, <b>ni</b>ckel, and <b>co</b>balt (p. 12-11).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Ferrous = iron-based; limited nonferrous magnetics = <b>nickel, cobalt,\ngadolinium</b>; repelled by both poles = <b>diamagnetic</b>; iron & alloys =\n<b>ferromagnetic</b> (p. 12-6/12-9).</li>\n<li>\"N pole\" = <b>north-seeking pole</b>; flux lines <b>never cross, repel each\nother</b>; no more flux fits = <b>saturated</b> (p. 12-6/12-8).</li>\n<li><b>Permeability</b> = ease of flux (vacuum = 1); <b>reluctance</b> = magnetic\nresistance (p. 12-9).</li>\n<li>Retentivity = holds magnetism; <b>residual magnetism</b> = leftover in soft\niron (generators depend on it); heat/hammer/drop = weaker; store with\n<b>keeper bars</b>; best permanent magnet = <b>Alnico</b> (p. 12-8…12-11).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Magnetism), pp. 12-6…12-11.</p>\n", "topics": ["Magnetism"], "quiz": [{"q": "Magnetic lines of force behave such that:", "options": ["They only exist in air", "They cross each other freely", "They never cross one another and actually repel each other", "They have no direction"], "correct": 2, "short": "They never cross and repel each other", "expl": "Lines of force are separate, individual forces; one line never crosses another — they actually repel one another and remain parallel.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "When storing a magnet, 'keeper bars' are installed across the poles to:", "options": ["Reverse the polarity", "Increase the magnet's heat", "Add electrical charge", "Provide an easy flux path and promote retention of magnetism"], "correct": 3, "short": "Provide an easy flux path / retain magnetism", "expl": "Keeper bars provide an easy path for flux lines from one pole to the other, promoting retention of the molecules' North-South alignment.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Which three nonferrous materials are magnetic to a limited degree?", "options": ["Silver, gold, lead", "Nickel, cobalt, and gadolinium", "Brass, bronze, zinc", "Copper, aluminum, tin"], "correct": 1, "short": "Nickel, cobalt, gadolinium", "expl": "Magnetic materials include at least three nonferrous materials magnetic to a limited degree: nickel, cobalt, and gadolinium.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-6", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Which alloy of iron, aluminum, nickel, and cobalt is considered one of the very best permanent-magnet materials?", "options": ["Manganin", "Constantan", "Alnico", "Nichrome"], "correct": 2, "short": "Alnico", "expl": "Alnico (aluminum-nickel-cobalt) is considered one of the very best permanent-magnet materials. Others include ferrites, neodymium-iron-boron, and samarium-cobalt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-11", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Iron and its alloys, which have by far the greatest permeability, are termed:", "options": ["Diamagnetic", "Nonmagnetic", "Paramagnetic", "Ferromagnetic"], "correct": 3, "short": "Ferromagnetic", "expl": "Ferromagnetic applies to iron and its alloys, which have by far the greatest permeability. Nonferrous metals with permeability greater than one (nickel, cobalt) are paramagnetic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-9", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "When no more lines of force can be induced into a piece of magnetic material, the material is said to be:", "options": ["Polarized", "Saturated", "Neutralized", "Demagnetized"], "correct": 1, "short": "Saturated", "expl": "There is a limit to the number of lines of force a magnet can hold; when no more can be induced, the material is saturated.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "The small amount of magnetism remaining in soft iron after the magnetizing force is removed — important to generator operation — is called:", "options": ["Coercive force", "Residual magnetism", "Permeability", "Saturation"], "correct": 1, "short": "Residual magnetism", "expl": "The small amount of magnetism remaining after the force is removed is residual magnetism, of great importance in such applications as generator operation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-11", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "The measure of the EASE with which magnetic flux can penetrate a material is called:", "options": ["Reluctance", "Coercivity", "Permeability", "Retentivity"], "correct": 2, "short": "Permeability", "expl": "Permeability is the measure of the ease with which magnetic flux can penetrate a material. Soft iron and ferrous metals have high permeability. A vacuum is rated 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-9", "topic": "Magnetism"}, {"q": "Heating, hammering, or dropping a magnet causes the strength of the magnet to:", "options": ["Decrease", "Reverse", "Increase", "Remain the same"], "correct": 0, "short": "Decrease", "expl": "Heating or sudden shock creates misalignment of the molecules, causing the magnet's strength to decrease.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-8", "topic": "Magnetism"}]}, {"id": "b4m3s2", "title": "Electromagnetism & solenoids", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> In 1820, <b>Oersted</b> noticed a compass needle deflect\nnext to a current-carrying wire — the discovery that <b>electricity and\nmagnetism are linked</b> (p. 12-12). Everything with a coil in it descends from\nthat one observation.</p>\n\n<h3>The field around a wire</h3>\n<p>Current through a straight conductor creates a magnetic field shaped as\n<b>a series of concentric circles around the conductor</b> — rings around a\npipe (p. 12-12). To find the field direction when you know the current\ndirection, the handbook uses the <b>LEFT-hand rule</b> (for electron flow):\ngrasp the wire, thumb pointing the way the electrons flow, and your curled\nfingers show the direction of the field circles (p. 12-12).</p>\n\n<h3>Coils and electromagnets</h3>\n<p>Loop that wire into a coil and the little circular fields stack into one\nstrong field. Slide a <b>soft-iron core</b> inside and you have an\n<b>electromagnet</b> (p. 12-13). The core does two things: it <b>increases the\nmagnetic flux AND concentrates the flux lines</b> (higher permeability path\nthan air) (p. 12-13).</p>\n<p><b>Coil polarity — left-hand rule for coils:</b> grasp the coil with the LEFT\nhand, fingers pointing in the direction of electron flow (minus to plus);\n<b>the thumb points to the NORTH pole</b> (p. 12-14).</p>\n<p><b>Field strength:</b> increase the current, or <b>increase the number of\nloops in the wire</b> (p. 12-14). (Amp-turns: both knobs multiply together.)</p>\n\n<h3>The solenoid — electromagnetism doing work</h3>\n<p>A <b>solenoid</b> — the handbook also calls it a <b>solenoid switch or\nrelay</b> — is a coil with a <b>spring-loaded iron core that gets pulled\nto center position when the coil is energized</b>, completing (or mechanically\nmoving) a circuit (p. 12-14). Energize: core slams in. De-energize: spring\nthrows it back. That's your starter solenoid — a tiny cockpit signal physically\nthrowing a heavy switch.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Oersted (1820)</b>: compass deflects near a current — electricity ↔\nmagnetism (p. 12-12).</li>\n<li>Field around a wire = <b>concentric circles</b>; direction by the\n<b>left-hand rule</b> (electron flow) (p. 12-12).</li>\n<li>Electromagnet = coil + soft-iron core; core <b>increases and concentrates</b>\nthe flux (p. 12-13).</li>\n<li>Coil left-hand rule: fingers = electron flow → <b>thumb = north pole</b>\n(p. 12-14).</li>\n<li>Stronger field: more current or <b>more loops</b> (p. 12-14).</li>\n<li><b>Solenoid</b> = spring-loaded iron core pulled to center when the coil\nenergizes (p. 12-14).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Electromagnetism, solenoids), pp. 12-12…12-14.</p>\n", "topics": ["Electromagnetism"], "quiz": [{"q": "A device in which a spring-loaded iron core is pulled to a center position to complete a circuit when a coil is energized is a:", "options": ["Rectifier", "Potentiometer", "Capacitor", "Solenoid (solenoid switch/relay)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid", "expl": "The solenoid (also called solenoid switch or relay) uses a spring-loaded off-center iron core that is rapidly pulled to center when the coil is energized, completing a circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "In 1820, who discovered that a compass needle is deflected near a current-carrying conductor, demonstrating a link between electricity and magnetism?", "options": ["Oersted", "Lenz", "Ohm", "Faraday"], "correct": 0, "short": "Oersted", "expl": "In 1820, Danish physicist Hans Christian Oersted discovered that a compass needle is deflected near a current-carrying conductor, linking electricity and magnetism.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-12", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "Per the handbook, which rule determines the direction of the magnetic field lines around a current-carrying conductor when the direction of current flow is known?", "options": ["Left-hand rule", "Right-hand rule", "Fleming's rule", "Lenz's rule"], "correct": 0, "short": "Left-hand rule", "expl": "Grasp the conductor in the LEFT hand with the thumb pointing in the direction of current flow; the fingers wrap in the direction of the magnetic field lines. This is the left-hand rule.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-12", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "The magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor takes what shape?", "options": ["A series of concentric circles around the conductor", "A single loop at the end", "A square pattern", "A straight line along the wire"], "correct": 0, "short": "Concentric circles around the conductor", "expl": "A series of concentric circles around the conductor represents the field; if all lines were shown it would appear as a continuous cylinder of circles.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-12", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "A coil of wire loops with a soft-iron core inside, which concentrates the magnetic flux, is called a(n):", "options": ["Rheostat", "Electromagnet", "Resistor", "Capacitor"], "correct": 1, "short": "Electromagnet", "expl": "An iron core in a coil of wire loops is an electromagnet; the soft iron (greater permeability than air) concentrates the flux in the center and the poles act like a bar magnet.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-13", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "The strength of an electromagnet's field can be increased by increasing the current flow or by:", "options": ["Cooling the core", "Increasing the number of loops in the wire", "Reversing the polarity", "Decreasing the number of loops"], "correct": 1, "short": "Increasing the number of loops", "expl": "Field strength is increased by increasing current flow or the number of loops; doubling either approximately doubles the field strength. The core metal type is also a factor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "Per the handbook, the polarity (North/South) of a current-carrying coil/electromagnet is found by grasping the coil with the LEFT hand so the fingers point in the direction of electron flow (minus to plus); the thumb then points to the:", "options": ["South pole", "Direction of flux only", "Center of the coil", "North pole"], "correct": 3, "short": "North pole", "expl": "Using the left-hand rule for a coil, with fingers curved in the direction of electron flow (minus to plus), the thumb points to the North pole.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-14", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}, {"q": "Adding a soft-iron core to a current-carrying coil does which TWO things?", "options": ["Reverses polarity and adds resistance", "Lowers voltage and raises current", "Decreases flux and disperses lines", "Increases the magnetic flux and concentrates the flux lines"], "correct": 3, "short": "Increases flux & concentrates lines", "expl": "The soft-iron core does two things: it increases the magnetic flux and concentrates the flux lines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-13", "topic": "Electromagnetism"}]}, {"id": "b4m3s3", "title": "Batteries & cells (lead-acid, Ni-Cad, Li-ion)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A battery is a chemical pressure tank: chemical energy in,\nelectrical pressure out. The ACS hits batteries hard (AM.I.A.K15, R2, R4 —\nhandling, charging, and servicing risks), and the written mines this section\nfor numbers. Take it slow; it's the densest section in the block.</p>\n\n<h3>Cells: primary vs secondary</h3>\n<p>The building block is the <b>cell</b>. If its energy-releasing chemical\nreaction is <b>NOT reversible</b>, it's a <b>primary cell</b> — use once, toss\n(a flashlight AA). If the reaction IS <b>reversible</b>, it's a <b>secondary\ncell</b> — rechargeable, which is what aircraft use (p. 12-87).</p>\n\n<h3>Lead-acid: the chemistry</h3>\n<p>Inside each lead-acid cell (p. 12-87): electrolyte = <b>sulfuric acid</b>\n(diluted), positive plate = <b>lead peroxide (lead dioxide)</b>, negative plate\n= <b>spongy lead</b>. A typical <b>12-volt battery = 6 cells in series</b>\n(p. 12-87), because each cell is rated at about <b>2 volts</b> (p. 12-89) —\nand reads about <b>2.2 volts fresh off the charger</b> (p. 12-89).</p>\n\n<h3>Capacity — the ampere-hour</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Capacity rating = <b>ampere-hours (Ah)</b>: amps multiplied by hours of\ndischarge (p. 12-89). A 1-Ah battery ideally gives 1 A for 1 hour — or ½ A for\n<b>2 hours</b> (p. 12-89).</li>\n<li>For AIRPLANE batteries, the rating basis is the <b>5-hour discharge\nperiod</b> (p. 12-89).</li>\n<li>Hookup rules: batteries in <b>PARALLEL → ampere-hour capacity adds</b>\n(voltage same); in <b>SERIES → the total voltage adds</b> (p. 12-89).</li>\n<li><b>Life cycle:</b> the number of charge/discharge cycles before capacity\ndrops below <b>80 percent</b> of initial rated capacity (p. 12-89).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The hydrometer — reading state of charge</h3>\n<p>A lead-acid battery's state of charge shows in the <b>density (specific\ngravity) of its electrolyte</b>, measured with a <b>hydrometer</b> (p. 12-90).\nThe numbers (all p. 12-90/12-91):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hydrometer scale range: <b>1.100 to 1.300</b>.</li>\n<li>New, fully charged: electrolyte ≈ <b>30% acid / 70% water</b>, specific\ngravity <b>1.270–1.300</b>. Discharged = low SG (the acid has moved into the\nplates).</li>\n<li><b>Temperature correction:</b> none needed while electrolyte is between\n<b>70 °F and 90 °F</b>; outside that window, apply the correction chart.</li>\n<li>Always take the reading <b>BEFORE adding distilled water</b> — water on top\nwould dilute the sample and fake a low reading.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Charging methods</h3>\n<p>Two standard methods: the <b>constant current</b> method and the <b>constant\nvoltage</b> method (p. 12-91). On the aircraft, the battery is charged by the\ngenerator system using the <b>constant voltage method</b> — the voltage\nregulator holds the bus voltage steady and the battery draws what it needs\n(p. 12-91).</p>\n<p><b>Safety:</b> a charging lead-acid battery generates an explosive mixture of\n<b>hydrogen and oxygen</b> — ventilate, no sparks (p. 12-91).</p>\n\n<h3>Nickel-cadmium (Ni-Cad)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Electrolyte: <b>potassium hydroxide (KOH)</b> — an alkaline, not an acid\n(p. 12-91). Typical open-circuit cell voltage ≈ <b>1.25 volts</b> (p. 12-92).</li>\n<li>Unlike lead-acid, the KOH's specific gravity does NOT track the charge —\nyou can't hydrometer-test a Ni-Cad's state of charge.</li>\n<li>Near full charge the cells emit gas; to completely charge a Ni-Cad, some\n<b>gassing must take place, however slight</b> (p. 12-92).</li>\n<li><b>Add water ONLY when fully charged</b> — the electrolyte level rises with\ncharge, so topping up a discharged Ni-Cad ends in overflow when charged\n(p. 12-92).</li>\n<li>Constant overcharge + infrequent discharge causes <b>voltage depression</b> —\ncommonly (and erroneously) called <b>memory effect</b> (p. 12-93).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Thermal runaway — exam-prep note</span>\nAIM classes and the written expect this term: in a Ni-Cad, overheating lowers\ninternal resistance, which raises charging current, which makes more heat — a\nself-feeding spiral called <b>thermal runaway</b> that can destroy the battery.\nThe current -30B Ch.12 text doesn't use the phrase (it covers the overcharge/\ngassing behavior above), so cite it from class notes — and flag it to your\ninstructor if a worksheet wants a handbook page.</div>\n\n<h3>Lithium-ion</h3>\n<p>Li-ion has reached transport aircraft: one of the first to use lithium-ion\nmain batteries is the <b>Boeing 787</b> (p. 12-93).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary cell = not reversible; secondary = reversible/rechargeable</b> (p. 12-87).</li>\n<li>Lead-acid: <b>sulfuric acid / lead peroxide (+) / spongy lead (−)</b>;\n<b>6 cells = 12 V</b>; ~2 V per cell rated, <b>2.2 V off the charger</b> (p. 12-87/12-89).</li>\n<li><b>Ampere-hour</b> capacity (1 Ah = ½ A for 2 h); airplane rating basis =\n<b>5-hour discharge</b>; parallel adds <b>Ah</b>, series adds <b>voltage</b>;\nlife cycle floor = <b>80%</b> (p. 12-89).</li>\n<li>Hydrometer: scale <b>1.100–1.300</b>; full charge <b>1.270–1.300</b>\n(30% acid/70% water); no temp correction <b>70–90 °F</b>; read <b>before adding\nwater</b> (p. 12-90/12-91).</li>\n<li>Charging: <b>constant current or constant voltage</b>; aircraft use\n<b>constant voltage</b> (regulator-held); charging gas = <b>hydrogen + oxygen</b>\n(p. 12-91).</li>\n<li>Ni-Cad: <b>KOH</b> electrolyte, <b>1.25 V/cell</b>, must gas slightly to top\noff, <b>water only when fully charged</b>, voltage depression = \"memory effect,\"\nthermal-runaway risk (p. 12-91…12-93).</li>\n<li>Li-ion pioneer aircraft = <b>Boeing 787</b> (p. 12-93).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Batteries and cells), pp. 12-87…12-93.</p>\n", "topics": ["Batteries & Cells"], "quiz": [{"q": "For a nickel-cadmium battery, water should be added to the cells only when the battery is:", "options": ["Fully charged", "Cold", "Fully discharged", "Half charged"], "correct": 0, "short": "Fully charged", "expl": "During discharge the plates absorb electrolyte; on recharge the level rises and is highest at full charge. Therefore water should be added only when the battery is fully charged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-92", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "In a lead-acid cell, the electrolyte is sulfuric acid, the positive electrode is lead peroxide (lead dioxide), and the negative electrode is:", "options": ["Lead (spongy lead)", "Carbon", "Zinc", "Copper"], "correct": 0, "short": "Lead (spongy lead)", "expl": "The lead-acid cell uses sulfuric acid electrolyte, positive plates of lead dioxide (PbO2), and negative plates of spongy lead.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-87", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "The two standard methods of charging a battery are the constant current method and the:", "options": ["Constant voltage method", "Specific-gravity method", "Constant resistance method", "Trickle-pulse method"], "correct": 0, "short": "Constant voltage method", "expl": "Batteries are charged by either the constant voltage or the constant current method. In constant voltage, charging current tapers off as the battery nears full charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-91", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "For rating airplane battery capacity, what discharge time period has been established as the basis?", "options": ["1 hour", "20 hours", "5 hours", "10 hours"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 hours", "expl": "For airplane batteries, a period of 5 hours has been established as the discharge time used in rating battery capacity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-89", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "How many lead-acid cells in series make up a typical 12-volt battery?", "options": ["3 cells", "6 cells", "12 cells", "24 cells"], "correct": 1, "short": "6 cells", "expl": "Each lead-acid cell produces approximately 2 volts; a 12-volt battery uses 6 cells in series, and a 24-volt battery uses 12 cells.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-87", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "When taking a hydrometer reading, no temperature correction is necessary as long as the electrolyte temperature is between:", "options": ["70 F and 90 F", "50 F and 70 F", "32 F and 50 F", "90 F and 110 F"], "correct": 0, "short": "70 F and 90 F", "expl": "No correction is necessary when the temperature is between 70 F and 90 F. Above 90 F or below 70 F, a correction factor must be applied.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-90", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "The undesirable Ni-Cad characteristic caused by constant overcharge and infrequent discharge, technically called 'voltage depression' but commonly (and erroneously) called:", "options": ["Thermal runaway", "Sulfation", "Memory effect", "Gassing"], "correct": 2, "short": "Memory effect", "expl": "Ni-Cads exhibit 'voltage depression,' commonly but erroneously called 'memory effect.' SLA batteries do not have this characteristic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "In a new, fully-charged aircraft battery, the electrolyte is approximately 30 percent acid and 70 percent water, with a specific gravity of about:", "options": ["1.270 to 1.300", "1.200", "1.150", "1.000"], "correct": 0, "short": "About 1.270 to 1.300", "expl": "A new fully-charged battery's electrolyte is ~30 percent acid/70 percent water and is about 1.300 (1.270 at 60 F). During discharge it drops below 1.300 (to ~1.150 discharged).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-90", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}, {"q": "One of the first aircraft to utilize lithium-ion batteries is the:", "options": ["Piper Cub", "Airbus A320", "Cessna 172", "Boeing 787"], "correct": 3, "short": "Boeing 787", "expl": "The FAA certified lithium-ion batteries for aircraft; one of the first to use them is the Boeing 787, which uses two 32V 8-cell lithium-ion batteries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Batteries & Cells"}]}, {"id": "b4m3s4", "title": "AC theory & values (RMS/peak)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> DC is a river — one direction, steady. AC is a tide —\nsloshing back and forth on a schedule. Aircraft use both, and AC won the power\ngrid for one reason the written asks directly: <b>AC voltage can be raised or\nlowered easily with transformers</b> (p. 12-43).</p>\n\n<h3>Where AC comes from — induction</h3>\n<p>In 1831, <b>Faraday</b> discovered that a magnetic field can create current in\na conductor — <b>electromagnetic induction</b> (p. 12-44). Move a wire through\na field (or a field past a wire) and voltage appears. <b>Lenz's Law</b> adds the\ndirection: the induced current always flows so that its own magnetic field\n<b>opposes the motion</b> that created it (p. 12-44) — nature pushes back.</p>\n<p>The <b>generator LEFT-hand rule</b> keeps the three directions straight:\nthumb = motion of the conductor, index finger = field (flux) direction, and the\n<b>SECOND finger = the direction of the induced emf</b> (p. 12-44).</p>\n<p>The <b>size</b> of the induced emf depends on three factors (p. 12-44):\n<b>the strength of the magnetic field, the speed of rotation, and the number of\nwires moving through the field</b>.</p>\n<p>Spin a loop in a field continuously and you get an AC generator — an\n<b>alternator</b> (p. 12-44) — with the current taken off the rotating loop by\nbrushes riding on two metal <b>slip rings</b> (p. 12-45). (Swap the slip rings\nfor a split ring and you get DC — Unit 4.)</p>\n\n<h3>Reading the sine wave</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>One complete pattern — up to max positive, back to zero, down to max\nnegative, back to zero — is a <b>cycle</b> (p. 12-46), containing <b>two\nalternations</b> (positive and negative) (p. 12-46). One full cycle spans\n<b>360 electrical degrees</b> (p. 12-49).</li>\n<li><b>Frequency</b> = cycles per second, unit the <b>hertz (Hz)</b> (p. 12-46).\nA 2-pole generator at 3,600 rpm = 3,600 ÷ 60 sec = <b>60 cps (60 Hz)</b>\n(p. 12-46). Aircraft AC systems and inverters run at <b>400 cps</b> — higher\nfrequency lets generators and transformers be smaller and lighter (p. 12-93).</li>\n<li><b>Period</b> = the time for one full cycle; <b>period = 1 ÷ frequency</b>\n(p. 12-46).</li>\n<li><b>Wavelength</b> (Greek letter <b>lambda</b>) = the distance the waveform\ntravels during one period (p. 12-47).</li>\n<li>Two waveforms peaking together = <b>in phase</b>; peaking at different times\n= out of phase, and the one peaking first <b>LEADS</b> the other (p. 12-47).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The three values of AC (p. 12-48/12-49)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Instantaneous value:</b> the voltage/current at any single instant during\nthe cycle — zero at 0°, max at 90°, zero again at 180°…</li>\n<li><b>Peak value:</b> the LARGEST instantaneous value — at <b>90°</b> positive\nand <b>270°</b> negative. Maximum = 1.41 × the effective value.</li>\n<li><b>Effective value = RMS (root mean square):</b> the AC value that does the\nsame heating work as an equal DC voltage. <b>RMS = 0.707 × peak.</b> AC meters\ndisplay RMS, and any AC value is <b>assumed to be effective (RMS) unless\notherwise specified</b> (p. 12-49).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked conversion (p. 12-49)</span>\nPeak from RMS: <b>Vp = 1.414 × Vrms</b>. Household 110 V RMS →\n110 × 1.41 ≈ <b>155 volts peak</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Legacy trap — average value</span>\nOlder FAA texts also defined an <b>average value = 0.637 × peak</b>. The current\n-30B lists only instantaneous/peak/RMS — but 0.637 still shows up on the\nwritten as a distractor. Remember: <b>0.707 = RMS (the answer they want);\n0.637 = average (the trap)</b>.</div>\n<p>Last connection: two effects exist in AC circuits that never occur in DC —\n<b>inductive reactance and capacitive reactance</b> (p. 12-44). They're the\nwhole story of Unit 4.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>AC won because of <b>transformers</b> (easy voltage change) (p. 12-43).</li>\n<li><b>Faraday 1831</b> = induction; <b>Lenz</b> = induced current opposes the\nmotion; generator left-hand rule: <b>2nd finger = induced emf</b> (p. 12-44).</li>\n<li>Induced emf depends on: field strength, speed, <b>number of wires</b> (p. 12-44).</li>\n<li>Alternator takes power off via brushes on <b>slip rings</b> (p. 12-45).</li>\n<li>Cycle = 2 alternations = <b>360°</b>; frequency in <b>hertz</b>; 2-pole @\n3,600 rpm = <b>60 cps</b>; aircraft standard = <b>400 cps</b>;\n<b>period = 1/f</b>; wavelength = <b>lambda</b>; first to peak <b>leads</b>\n(p. 12-46/12-47, 12-93).</li>\n<li>Three AC values: <b>instantaneous, peak (90°/270°), effective (RMS)</b>;\n<b>RMS = 0.707 × peak; Vp = 1.414 × Vrms</b> (110 V → 155 V);\nunspecified values are RMS; 0.637 = legacy average = trap (p. 12-48/12-49).</li>\n<li>AC-only effects: <b>inductive & capacitive reactance</b> (p. 12-44).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (AC generation, sine-wave values), pp. 12-43…12-49.</p>\n", "topics": ["AC Theory", "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"], "quiz": [{"q": "The period of a sine wave and its frequency are related how?", "options": ["Period = 1 / frequency", "Period = 2 x frequency", "Period = frequency squared", "Period = frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Period = 1 / frequency", "expl": "Period is inversely proportional to frequency: t = 1/f, and f = 1/t. The higher the frequency, the shorter the period.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-46", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The distance a waveform travels during one period, indicated by the Greek letter lambda, is the:", "options": ["Period", "Wavelength", "Amplitude", "Frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Wavelength", "expl": "Wavelength (lambda) is the distance a waveform travels during a period, measured from one point on the waveform to the corresponding point on the next.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-47", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The three values of AC are instantaneous, peak, and:", "options": ["Apparent", "Effective (RMS)", "Average", "Reactive"], "correct": 1, "short": "Effective (RMS)", "expl": "There are three values of AC: instantaneous, peak, and effective root mean square (RMS).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-48", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "Which two effects occur in AC circuits that do NOT occur in DC circuits?", "options": ["Resistance and power", "Polarity and frequency", "Voltage drop and current flow", "Inductive reactance and capacitive reactance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Inductive & capacitive reactance", "expl": "Because AC constantly changes direction and intensity, inductive reactance and capacitive reactance occur in AC circuits but not in DC circuits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "The value of an induced emf depends on three factors: the strength of the magnetic field, the speed of rotation, and:", "options": ["The wire color", "The number of wires moving through the field", "The air temperature", "The wire resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Number of wires in the field", "expl": "Induced emf depends on (1) number of wires moving through the field, (2) strength of the magnetic field, and (3) speed of rotation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "Household AC is 110 volts RMS. Approximately what is the PEAK voltage?", "options": ["110 volts", "78 volts", "155 volts", "220 volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "Approximately 155 volts", "expl": "Vp = 1.414 x 110 = ~155 volts. The handbook gives this exact example: the 110 V supplied to homes is only 0.707 of the ~155 V peak.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "The largest instantaneous value of a sine wave, occurring at 90 degrees (and 270 degrees in the opposite direction), is the:", "options": ["Peak value", "Average value", "Instantaneous value", "RMS value"], "correct": 0, "short": "Peak value", "expl": "The peak value is the largest instantaneous value; the largest positive value occurs at 90 degrees and the largest negative at 270 degrees.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-48", "topic": "AC Values (RMS/Peak)"}, {"q": "Using the generator LEFT-hand rule, the SECOND finger of the left hand points in the direction of the:", "options": ["Movement of the conductor", "Magnetic lines of force", "Induced emf", "Current in the field coil"], "correct": 2, "short": "Induced emf", "expl": "Generator left-hand rule: first finger = magnetic lines of force (N to S), thumb = movement of conductor, second finger = direction of induced emf.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-44", "topic": "AC Theory"}, {"q": "How many electrical degrees are there in one complete cycle of an AC sine wave?", "options": ["360 degrees", "270 degrees", "90 degrees", "180 degrees"], "correct": 0, "short": "360 degrees", "expl": "One full sine-wave cycle spans 360 degrees: zero at 0, max at 90, zero at 180, max (opposite) at 270, zero at 360.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Theory"}]}]}, {"n": 4, "day": "Unit 4", "title": "AC Components, Machines & Electronics", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.12 · Reactance, machines, solid state · ACS AM.I.A.K3, K4, K9a, K11a, K16, K21-K23, K27", "sections": [{"id": "b4m4s1", "title": "Capacitance & inductance", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Two components store energy and fight <b>change</b>.\nThe <b>capacitor</b> is a stretchy membrane across the pipe — it stretches to\nabsorb pressure changes (opposes a change in VOLTAGE). The <b>inductor</b> is a\nheavy flywheel in the flow — hard to spin up, hard to stop (opposes a change in\nCURRENT). Burn that pairing in; half of Unit 4 hangs on it.</p>\n\n<h3>The capacitor</h3>\n<p>Two parallel plates separated by a nonconductor called the <b>dielectric</b>\n(p. 12-50). Charge piles up on the plates and is stored in the electric field\nbetween them. Once charged from a DC source, a good capacitor <b>blocks DC but\npasses the effects of pulsing DC or AC</b> (p. 12-50).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unit: the <b>farad</b> — <b>one coulomb of charge stored at one volt</b>\nacross the plates (p. 12-50). A whole farad is huge, so real parts use the\n<b>microfarad and picofarad</b> (p. 12-51).</li>\n<li>Three factors set capacitance (p. 12-51): <b>plate AREA (directly\nproportional)</b>, <b>plate SPACING (inversely proportional)</b>, and the\n<b>dielectric material</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Voltage rating</b> (breakdown/working voltage): the max DC voltage the\ncapacitor can take without arcing through (p. 12-51). Pick a working voltage at\nleast <b>50 percent greater</b> than the highest applied voltage (p. 12-51).</li>\n<li>The <b>electrolytic</b> capacitor is <b>polarized</b> (marked + and −) —\nDC or pulsating DC ONLY; reverse it and it can fail violently (p. 12-52).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The RC time constant</h3>\n<p>Charging isn't instant. The <b>time constant (the Greek letter tau, τ) of an\nRC circuit equals the resistance times the capacitance: τ = R × C</b>\n(p. 12-50). In <b>one</b> time constant the capacitor charges to about\n<b>63 percent</b> of source voltage; after <b>5 time constants</b> it's at about\n<b>99 percent</b> — call it fully charged (p. 12-50).</p>\n\n<h3>Combining capacitors — backwards from resistors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>PARALLEL: capacitances ADD directly</b> — CT = C1 + C2 + …\nExample: 330 µF + 220 µF = <b>550 µF</b> (p. 12-53). (More plate area.)</li>\n<li><b>SERIES: total is LESS than the smallest capacitor</b> (reciprocal\nformula — more spacing) (p. 12-53).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The inductor</h3>\n<p><b>Inductance</b> is the property of a coil to <b>oppose any CHANGE in\ncurrent</b> through it (p. 12-56), stored as a magnetic field. The law behind\nit: <b>Faraday's Law</b> — the induced emf in a closed loop is proportional to\nthe <b>rate of change of the magnetic flux</b> through the coil (p. 12-56).\n<b>Lenz's Law (1833)</b> gives its direction: the induced emf always\n<b>opposes the change in flux</b> that made it (p. 12-57).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unit: the <b>henry</b> (H). The definition mirrors the flywheel picture: a\ncircuit has an inductance of <b>one henry when current changing at the rate of\none ampere per second induces one volt</b> into the circuit (p. 12-58;\n-30B Glossary).</li>\n<li>An RL circuit has a time constant too: current reaches <b>63 percent</b> of\nfull value in 1 time constant (p. 12-56) — same 63/99 rhythm as RC.</li>\n<li>Physical factors (p. 12-57): inductance varies as the <b>SQUARE of the\nnumber of turns</b> (double the turns ≈ 4× L); <b>doubling the coil's LENGTH</b>\nwith the same turns <b>HALVES</b> the inductance.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Combining inductors — like resistors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SERIES: inductances ADD — the total is the SUM of the individual\ninductors</b>, LT = L1 + L2 + … (p. 12-58).</li>\n<li><b>PARALLEL: total is LESS than the smallest inductance</b> (p. 12-58).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">The combining table</span>\n<b>Resistors & inductors:</b> series adds, parallel shrinks below the smallest.<br>\n<b>Capacitors:</b> the OPPOSITE — parallel adds, series shrinks below the smallest.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Capacitor = plates + <b>dielectric</b>; <b>blocks DC, passes AC</b>;\nfarad = <b>1 coulomb at 1 volt</b>; practical units µF & <b>picofarad</b>\n(p. 12-50/12-51).</li>\n<li>Capacitance ∝ <b>plate area</b>, inverse to <b>spacing</b>, set by\n<b>dielectric</b>; working voltage ≥ <b>150%</b> of applied; <b>electrolytic =\npolarized, DC only</b> (p. 12-51/12-52).</li>\n<li><b>TC = R × C</b>; 1 TC = <b>63%</b>, 5 TC ≈ <b>99%</b> (RC and RL both)\n(p. 12-50/12-56).</li>\n<li>Caps: <b>parallel add (330+220 = 550 µF), series &lt; smallest</b>.\nInductors: <b>series add, parallel &lt; smallest</b> (p. 12-53/12-58).</li>\n<li>Inductance opposes <b>current change</b>; unit <b>henry</b>; L ∝ turns²;\ndouble length = half L; <b>Faraday = rate of flux change; Lenz = opposes the\nchange</b> (p. 12-56…12-58).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Capacitance, inductance, time constants), pp. 12-50…12-58.</p>\n", "topics": ["Capacitance & Inductance"], "quiz": [{"q": "The basic unit of inductance is the:", "options": ["Ohm", "Henry", "Farad", "Coulomb"], "correct": 1, "short": "Henry", "expl": "The henry (H) is the basic unit of inductance. A circuit has one henry when current changing at one ampere per second induces one volt.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Which capacitor type is generally POLARIZED (marked + and -) and should be used only with DC or pulsating DC?", "options": ["Air capacitor", "Ceramic capacitor", "Electrolytic capacitor", "Mica capacitor"], "correct": 2, "short": "Electrolytic capacitor", "expl": "Electrolytic capacitors are generally polarized (+ and - leads marked) and should be subjected to direct or pulsating direct voltage only. They provide large capacitance in small size.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-52", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "The two more common practical units of capacitance, smaller than the farad, are the microfarad and the:", "options": ["Henry", "Picofarad", "Kilofarad", "Megafarad"], "correct": 1, "short": "Picofarad", "expl": "Because one farad is large, smaller units are used: the microfarad (10^-6 F) and the picofarad (10^-12 F).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-51", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "One farad is defined as one coulomb of charge stored with how much voltage across the plates?", "options": ["Ten volts", "One hundred volts", "One volt", "One thousand volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "One volt", "expl": "By definition, one farad is one coulomb of charge stored with one volt across the plates of the capacitor (C = Q/E).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-50", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "In a series RL circuit, the current increases to what percentage of full value in 1 time constant after the circuit is closed?", "options": ["50 percent", "63 percent", "99 percent", "86 percent"], "correct": 1, "short": "63 percent", "expl": "In a series RL circuit, current increases to 63 percent of full value in 1 time constant, and reaches 99 percent after the 5th time constant — like an RC charging curve.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-56", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "A capacitor reaches approximately 99 percent of full charge after how many RC time constants?", "options": ["2", "3", "1", "5"], "correct": 3, "short": "5 time constants", "expl": "The time to reach about 99 percent of source voltage is approximately 5 time constants (5 tau).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-50", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "When inductors are connected in PARALLEL, the total inductance is:", "options": ["Equal to the largest", "The product of the inductors", "The sum of the inductors", "Less than the smallest inductance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Less than the smallest", "expl": "When inductors are connected in parallel, the total inductance is less than the smallest inductance (uses the reciprocal formula, like resistors in parallel).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Capacitors connected in PARALLEL combine how (to find total capacitance)?", "options": ["Use the reciprocal formula", "Take the smallest value", "Add them directly (CT = C1 + C2 + ...)", "Take the product over sum"], "correct": 2, "short": "Add them directly", "expl": "Capacitors in parallel add directly: CT = C1 + C2 + C3... (the effective plate area increases). This is OPPOSITE to resistors in parallel — a common trap.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-53", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}, {"q": "Faraday's Law (the Law of Electromagnetic Induction) states the induced emf in a closed loop of wire is proportional to:", "options": ["The wire color", "The rate of change of the magnetic flux through the coil", "The applied DC voltage", "The wire resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Rate of change of magnetic flux", "expl": "Faraday's Law: the induced emf in a closed loop of wire is proportional to the rate of change of the magnetic flux through a coil of wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-56", "topic": "Capacitance & Inductance"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s2", "title": "Reactance, impedance & power factor", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> In AC, the flywheel (inductor) and the membrane\n(capacitor) push back on the alternating flow. That pushback is\n<b>reactance</b> — like resistance, measured in ohms, but it changes with\n<b>frequency</b>. Combine reactance with plain resistance and you get\n<b>impedance</b>, the AC version of the pinch.</p>\n\n<h3>The two reactances</h3>\n<p>Three things oppose current in an AC circuit: resistance (same as DC), plus\ntwo effects that exist ONLY in AC — <b>inductive reactance and capacitive\nreactance</b> (p. 12-49).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Inductive reactance X<sub>L</sub> = 2πfL</b>, in <b>ohms</b> (p. 12-58).\nFrequency UP → X<sub>L</sub> <b>INCREASES</b> (faster changes, harder flywheel fight).\nWorked example: L = 0.146 H at 60 cps → X<sub>L</sub> = 2π × 60 × 0.146 ≈\n<b>55 ohms</b> (p. 12-58). In series, inductive reactances are <b>added like resistances in series</b>\n(p. 12-59).</li>\n<li><b>Capacitive reactance X<sub>C</sub> = 1 ÷ (2πfC)</b>, in ohms (p. 12-54).\nFrequency UP → X<sub>C</sub> <b>DECREASES</b> (the membrane passes fast wiggles\neasily). Capacitive reactances of capacitors in SERIES are found by <b>adding\nthe individual reactances</b> (p. 12-55).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Phase:</b> in a purely capacitive circuit, the current <b>LEADS the applied\nvoltage by 90°</b> (p. 12-56); in a purely inductive circuit voltage leads\ncurrent. Memory hook: <b>\"ELI the ICE man\"</b> — in an <b>L</b>, E before I;\nin a <b>C</b>, I before E.</p>\n\n<h3>Impedance — the total AC opposition</h3>\n<p><b>Impedance (Z)</b> is the combined total opposition to current flow in an\nAC circuit — resistance and reactance together (p. 12-59). Ohm's Law for AC\njust swaps R for Z: <b>I = E ÷ Z</b> (p. 12-59).</p>\n<p>Because reactance is 90° out of step with resistance, they add like the two\nlegs of a right triangle: <b>square each, add them, then take the square root\nof the sum</b> — the √ symbol below just means \"square root\" (p. 12-60):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>R and X<sub>L</sub>:</b> Z = √(R² + X<sub>L</sub>²). Handbook example:\nR = 6 Ω, X<sub>L</sub> = 8 Ω → Z = √(36+64) = <b>10 ohms</b> (p. 12-60).</li>\n<li><b>R and X<sub>C</sub>:</b> Z = √(R² + X<sub>C</sub>²). Example: R = 10 Ω,\nX<sub>C</sub> = 13 Ω → Z = √(100+169) ≈ <b>16.4 ohms</b> (p. 12-61).</li>\n<li><b>R, L, AND C together:</b> first net out the reactances — <b>subtract the\nsmaller from the larger: X = X<sub>L</sub> − X<sub>C</sub></b> (they pull in\nopposite phase directions) (p. 12-60). Example: R = 4, X<sub>L</sub> = 10,\nX<sub>C</sub> = 7 → X = 3 → Z = √(16+9) = <b>5 ohms</b> (p. 12-62).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At the special frequency where <b>X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub></b>, the two\ncancel completely and the circuit is <b>in RESONANCE</b> (at its resonant\nfrequency) — only R remains (p. 12-63). That's how radios tune.</p>\n\n<h3>True power, apparent power, power factor</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>True power</b> = the power actually consumed by the RESISTANCE — the real\nwork, what a wattmeter indicates, in watts (p. 12-64).</li>\n<li><b>Apparent power</b> = power apparently consumed by the ENTIRE circuit\n(resistance + reactance) = volts × amps, in <b>volt-amperes (VA)</b> (p. 12-64).</li>\n<li><b>Power factor = true power ÷ apparent power</b>, usually expressed as a\npercent (p. 12-64). True = apparent ONLY when the circuit is <b>pure\nresistance</b> (power factor 100%) (p. 12-64).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example (p. 12-65)</span>\nA 220-V AC motor draws 50 A; the wattmeter reads 9,350 W.<br>\nApparent power = 220 × 50 = <b>11,000 volt-amperes</b>.<br>\nPower factor = 9,350 ÷ 11,000 = <b>85 percent</b>.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>AC-only opposition: <b>X<sub>L</sub> = 2πfL rises</b> with f (0.146 H @ 60 cps\n≈ 55 Ω); <b>X<sub>C</sub> = 1/(2πfC) falls</b> with f; both in ohms; series\nreactances add (p. 12-54…12-59).</li>\n<li>Pure C: current <b>leads</b> voltage by 90° (<b>ICE</b>); pure L: voltage\nleads (<b>ELI</b>) (p. 12-56).</li>\n<li><b>Z</b> = total AC opposition; <b>I = E/Z</b>; Z = √(R² + X²); RLC:\n<b>X = X<sub>L</sub> − X<sub>C</sub></b> first (4/10/7 → Z = 5 Ω); 6-8 → 10 Ω;\n10-13 → 16.4 Ω (p. 12-59…12-62).</li>\n<li><b>Resonance: X<sub>L</sub> = X<sub>C</sub></b> (they cancel) (p. 12-63).</li>\n<li><b>PF = true ÷ apparent</b> (watts ÷ VA, as %); pure resistance → true =\napparent; 220 V × 50 A = 11,000 VA with 9,350 W → <b>85%</b> (p. 12-64/12-65).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Reactance, impedance, resonance, power factor), pp. 12-49…12-65.</p>\n", "topics": ["AC Reactance", "Impedance & Power Factor"], "quiz": [{"q": "For a series RLC circuit with R = 4 ohms, XL = 10 ohms, XC = 7 ohms, the impedance Z is:", "options": ["3 ohms", "21 ohms", "5 ohms", "11 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 ohms", "expl": "Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL - XC)^2) = sqrt(4^2 + (10-7)^2) = sqrt(16 + 9) = sqrt(25) = 5 ohms. Handbook worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-62", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "Besides resistance, which TWO factors oppose current flow ONLY in AC circuits (not DC)?", "options": ["Voltage and current", "Inductive reactance and capacitive reactance", "Permeability and reluctance", "Impedance and resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "Inductive & capacitive reactance", "expl": "Resistance opposes both AC and DC. Inductive reactance (XL) and capacitive reactance (XC) oppose current only in AC circuits and can create a phase shift.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-49", "topic": "AC Reactance"}, {"q": "The combined total opposition to current flow in an AC circuit, made up of resistance and reactance, is called:", "options": ["Conductance", "Capacitance", "Inductance", "Impedance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Impedance", "expl": "The combined effects of resistance, inductive reactance, and capacitive reactance make up the total opposition called impedance (Z), measured in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-59", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "A 220-volt AC motor draws 50 amperes; a wattmeter reads 9,350 watts. What is the apparent power?", "options": ["11,000 volt-amperes", "9,350 watts", "270 VA", "4,675 VA"], "correct": 0, "short": "11,000 volt-amperes", "expl": "Apparent power = volts x amperes = 220 x 50 = 11,000 watts (volt-amperes). This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "Ohm's Law for an AC circuit is written as:", "options": ["I = E / R", "I = E x Z", "I = E / Z", "I = Z / E"], "correct": 2, "short": "I = E / Z", "expl": "For AC, Ohm's Law uses impedance: I = E/Z. When the circuit is resistance only, Z = R and it is the same as the DC form.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-59", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "The ratio of true power to apparent power, usually expressed as a percent, is called the:", "options": ["Power factor", "Efficiency", "Coefficient of coupling", "Turns ratio"], "correct": 0, "short": "Power factor", "expl": "Power factor (PF) is the ratio of true power to apparent power, usually expressed in percent: PF = (watts / volt-amperes) x 100.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-64", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "In the handbook example, an inductance of 0.146 H at 60 cps gives an inductive reactance (XL = 2 pi f L) of approximately:", "options": ["11 ohms", "110 ohms", "55 ohms", "8 ohms"], "correct": 2, "short": "55 ohms", "expl": "XL = 6.28 x 60 x 0.146 = 55 ohms. This is the handbook's worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-58", "topic": "AC Reactance"}, {"q": "When a circuit contains resistance, inductance, AND capacitance, the reactances are combined by:", "options": ["Multiplying XL and XC", "Adding XL and XC", "Ignoring the smaller one", "Subtracting the smaller reactance from the larger (X = XL - XC)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Subtracting (X = XL - XC)", "expl": "Because their effects are exactly opposite, inductive and capacitive reactance are combined by subtraction (smaller from larger): then Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL - XC)^2).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-60", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}, {"q": "The power consumed by the ENTIRE AC circuit (both resistance and reactance), measured in volt-amps, is called:", "options": ["True power", "Working power", "Real power", "Apparent power"], "correct": 3, "short": "Apparent power", "expl": "Apparent power (symbol S, measured in volt-amps) is the power consumed by the entire circuit, including resistance and reactance — the product of effective voltage and current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-64", "topic": "Impedance & Power Factor"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s3", "title": "Transformers", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A transformer is two coils sharing a magnetic field —\na gearbox for voltage. It changes electrical energy from one voltage to another\nby <b>mutual induction between two coils</b> (p. 12-65), trading voltage for\ncurrent like gears trade speed for torque.</p>\n\n<h3>Why AC only</h3>\n<p>A transformer <b>cannot run on pure, steady DC</b>: only a <b>varying</b>\nvoltage creates the varying magnetic field needed for mutual induction\n(p. 12-65). Pure DC produces no varying field, and a steady field induces\nnothing — induction needs CHANGE (Faraday, again).</p>\n\n<h3>Parts and ratio</h3>\n<p>Three basic parts (p. 12-65): an <b>iron core</b>, a <b>primary winding</b>\n(input), and a <b>secondary winding</b> (output). What makes it step-up or\nstep-down is the <b>TURNS RATIO</b> — the voltage ratio equals the turns ratio\n(p. 12-65).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples (p. 12-65)</span>\n<b>Step-down 5:1</b> fed 10 V on the primary → secondary = 10 ÷ 5 = <b>2 volts</b>.<br>\n<b>Step-up 1:4</b> fed 10 V → secondary = 10 × 4 = <b>40 volts</b>.</div>\n<p>Energy is conserved: if a transformer steps voltage <b>UP</b>, it steps the\ncurrent <b>DOWN by the same ratio</b> (p. 12-66). No free power.</p>\n\n<h3>Losses — why efficiency isn't 100%</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Leakage flux:</b> some magnetic flux escapes the core and never cuts the\nsecondary (p. 12-66). Coupling example: primary makes 10,000 lines of force,\nonly 9,000 cut the secondary → coefficient of coupling/efficiency =\n<b>90 percent</b> (p. 12-66).</li>\n<li><b>Iron (core) losses,</b> two kinds (p. 12-67): <b>eddy current loss</b>\n(stray currents swirling in the core — reduced by building the core of\n<b>insulated laminations</b> — thin sheets coated with insulation) and <b>hysteresis loss</b> (energy\nspent re-flipping the core's magnetism every cycle).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Instrument transformers</h3>\n<p>Two specialized types feed meters in AC systems (p. 12-65):\n<b>current transformers are connected in SERIES</b> with the line; <b>voltage\n(potential) transformer primaries connect in PARALLEL</b> — exactly matching the\nammeter-series / voltmeter-parallel rule you already know.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Transformer = <b>mutual induction</b> between two coils; <b>AC only</b>\n(needs a varying field) (p. 12-65).</li>\n<li>Parts: <b>iron core, primary, secondary</b>; step-up vs step-down set by the\n<b>turns ratio</b>; 5:1 step-down, 10 V → <b>2 V</b>; 1:4 step-up, 10 V →\n<b>40 V</b> (p. 12-65).</li>\n<li>Voltage up = <b>current down</b> by the same ratio (p. 12-66).</li>\n<li>Losses: <b>leakage flux</b> (9,000/10,000 = <b>90%</b> coupling); iron losses\n= <b>eddy current</b> (fight with <b>laminations</b>) + <b>hysteresis</b>\n(p. 12-66/12-67).</li>\n<li>Instrument transformers: <b>current = series; voltage = parallel</b> (p. 12-65).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Transformers), pp. 12-65…12-67.</p>\n", "topics": ["Transformers"], "quiz": [{"q": "No transformer is 100 percent efficient because some of the magnetic flux escapes the magnetic circuit. This lost flux is called:", "options": ["Hysteresis flux", "Mutual flux", "Leakage flux", "Eddy flux"], "correct": 2, "short": "Leakage flux", "expl": "A certain amount of magnetic flux, called leakage flux, leaks out of the magnetic circuit; thus no transformer is 100 percent efficient (though iron-core types approach it).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-66", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "The factor that determines whether a transformer is step-up or step-down is the:", "options": ["Core material", "Turns ratio", "Wire gauge", "Frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Turns ratio", "expl": "The turns ratio — primary turns to secondary turns — determines step-up vs. step-down. The voltage ratio equals the turns ratio in an ideal transformer.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "A step-down transformer with a 5-to-1 turns ratio, fed with 10 volts on the primary, produces what secondary voltage?", "options": ["5 volts", "50 volts", "10 volts", "2 volts"], "correct": 3, "short": "2 volts", "expl": "Voltage ratio = turns ratio. A 5:1 step-down with 10 V on the primary gives 10/5 = 2 V on the secondary. Handbook example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "A transformer's primary develops 10,000 lines of force but only 9,000 cut the secondary. The transformer efficiency (coefficient of coupling) is:", "options": ["100 percent", "90 percent", "80 percent", "10 percent"], "correct": 1, "short": "90 percent", "expl": "9,000 / 10,000 = 0.9, so the coefficient of coupling is 0.9 and the transformer is 90 percent efficient.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-66", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "The 'iron' losses in a transformer are of two types: eddy current loss and:", "options": ["Hysteresis loss", "Leakage loss", "Resistive loss", "Copper loss"], "correct": 0, "short": "Hysteresis loss", "expl": "Iron losses are hysteresis loss (energy to re-magnetize the core each cycle) and eddy current loss (currents induced in the core). Copper loss is a separate conductor loss.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-67", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "A transformer changes electrical energy from one voltage to another by means of:", "options": ["Mutual induction between two coils", "Mechanical gearing", "Chemical reaction", "Direct electrical connection"], "correct": 0, "short": "Mutual induction", "expl": "A transformer's two coils are not electrically connected; an alternating voltage on one coil creates a voltage in the other by mutual induction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "A transformer has a 1-to-4 turns ratio (step-up). If 10 volts are applied to the primary, what is the secondary output?", "options": ["2.5 volts", "14 volts", "10 volts", "40 volts"], "correct": 3, "short": "40 volts", "expl": "Voltage ratio = turns ratio. With a 1:4 step-up and 10 V primary, secondary = 10 x 4 = 40 V. Handbook example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "The three basic parts of a transformer are an iron core, a primary winding, and a:", "options": ["Commutator", "Slip ring", "Brush assembly", "Secondary winding"], "correct": 3, "short": "Secondary winding", "expl": "A transformer has three basic parts: an iron core (low-reluctance flux path), a primary winding (receives source energy), and a secondary winding (receives energy by induction).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}, {"q": "Current transformers are connected how in the circuit, and voltage transformers how?", "options": ["Both in parallel", "Current transformers in parallel; voltage transformers in series", "Both in series", "Current transformers in series; voltage transformer primaries in parallel"], "correct": 3, "short": "Current=series; voltage=parallel", "expl": "Voltage transformer primaries are connected in PARALLEL across the supply voltage; current transformer primaries are connected in SERIES in the primary circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-65", "topic": "Transformers"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s4", "title": "Motors, generators & voltage regulation", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> One machine, run two directions: spin a coil in a\nmagnetic field and electricity comes out (<b>generator</b>); push current into\nthat coil and motion comes out (<b>motor</b>). This is the biggest ACS topic in\nthe unit (AM.I.A.K27 — AC and DC motors; K9a — voltage regulation), and the\nwritten mines it hard.</p>\n\n<h3>From AC loop to DC generator</h3>\n<p>The basic spinning loop makes AC, collected by brushes riding on <b>slip rings (collector rings)</b>\n(p. 12-45). <b>Replace the slip rings with two half-cylinders — a\ncommutator — and you have a basic DC generator</b>: the commutator flips the\nconnection every half turn, so the output always leaves in the same direction\n(p. 12-125). The rotating parts — coil plus commutator — are together called\nthe <b>armature</b> (p. 12-125).</p>\n\n<h3>The right-hand MOTOR rule & torque</h3>\n<p>A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field feels a push. The <b>right-hand\nmotor rule</b>: index finger = <b>field</b> direction, second finger =\n<b>current</b>, and the <b>thumb shows the direction the wire MOVES</b>\n(p. 12-138). (Generators use the left-hand rules; motors use the right.)</p>\n<p>Torque developed in the coil depends on three things (p. 12-138): field\nstrength, number of turns, and <b>the position of the coil in the field</b> —\nzero torque with the coil plane parallel to the flux, 100% when cutting at\nright angles. The commutator's job in a motor is to reverse the coil current at\njust the right moment so torque never dies (p. 12-139).</p>\n\n<h3>DC motors — the three types</h3>\n<p>Three basic types, named for how the field winding connects (p. 12-141):\n<b>series, shunt, and compound</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series motor:</b> field windings in SERIES with the armature →\n<b>very high starting torque</b> — the classic <b>aircraft engine starter</b>\n(p. 12-141). Its danger: <b>remove the load entirely and it can overspeed until\nthe armature may fly apart</b> (p. 12-142).</li>\n<li><b>Shunt motor:</b> field in PARALLEL (shunt) with the armature →\n<b>fairly constant speed</b> under varying load (p. 12-142).</li>\n<li><b>Compound motor:</b> <b>combines a series winding and a shunt winding</b> —\na compromise of both traits (p. 12-142).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Counter emf:</b> as the armature spins in the field, it generates its own\nvoltage that OPPOSES the applied voltage (p. 12-142) — the motor is also a\nreluctant generator. As armature speed <b>increases</b>, counter emf rises and\narmature current <b>DECREASES</b> (p. 12-142). That's why motors draw huge\ncurrent at start (no counter emf yet) and settle down at speed.</p>\n\n<h3>AC motors</h3>\n<p>Two general types are used in aircraft systems (p. 12-147): <b>induction\nmotors and synchronous motors</b> (either one single-, two-, or three-phase).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The three-phase <b>induction motor</b> — also called the <b>squirrel cage\nmotor</b> for its rotor of bars and end rings (p. 12-148) — handles the big\nloads: starters, flaps, landing gear, hydraulic pumps (p. 12-147). The stator's\nrotating field turns at <b>synchronous speed</b> (60 cps, 2-pole → 3,600 rpm)\n(p. 12-148), and the rotor always turns slightly slower — that necessary speed\ndifference is <b>SLIP</b>: without it, no lines of force would be cut and no\nrotor current induced (p. 12-149).</li>\n<li><b>Synchronous motors</b> run locked at constant synchronous speed — flux\ngate compasses, prop synchronizers (p. 12-147).</li>\n<li>The <b>AC-series motor</b> has brushes and a commutator like a DC motor and\nruns on <b>either AC or DC</b> (p. 12-152) — the \"universal motor.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alternator frequency depends on two things: <b>the number of poles and the\nspeed of rotation of the rotor</b> (p. 12-157).</p>\n\n<h3>Voltage regulation (ACS AM.I.A.K9a)</h3>\n<p>Generator output is controlled at the FIELD: <b>vary the resistance in the\nfield circuit</b> (a rheostat) → field current changes → field strength changes\n→ output voltage changes (p. 12-159). More field resistance = lower voltage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>vibrating-type regulator</b> automates that rheostat: contacts\nrapidly short/reinsert the field rheostat — voltage rises above the critical\nvalue, the solenoid pulls the contacts <b>open, reinserting the rheostat and\nreducing field current and terminal voltage</b>; voltage sags, the spring\nrecloses them. The rapid cycle holds a steady average from no load to full load\n(p. 12-160).</li>\n<li>Vibrating contacts <b>pit and burn</b> with high field current, so\nheavy-duty systems use the <b>CARBON PILE regulator</b> — a stack of carbon\ndisks whose resistance varies with compression (p. 12-161).</li>\n<li>Many light aircraft use the <b>THREE-UNIT regulator</b>: (1) a voltage\nregulator, (2) a <b>current limiter</b> (caps generator output current), and\n(3) a <b>reverse current cut-out</b> that disconnects the battery from the\ngenerator (p. 12-161). Without it, when generator voltage drops below battery\nvoltage, the battery discharges backward through the armature and drives the\ngenerator as a motor — called <b>\"MOTORING\" the generator</b> — and drains\nitself in short order (p. 12-161).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Which DC generator types exist? <b>Series wound, shunt wound, and compound\n(shunt-series) wound</b>, by field-to-load connection (p. 12-132). The SERIES\ngenerator has <b>very poor voltage regulation under changing load</b> (load\ncurrent IS field current), so it is <b>never used as an airplane generator</b> —\nairplane generators are shunt or compound (p. 12-132/12-133).</p>\n\n<h3>Inverters — DC back to AC</h3>\n<p>An <b>inverter</b> converts part of the aircraft's DC power to AC for\ninstruments, radio, radar, and lighting (p. 12-93), usually at <b>400 cps</b>\n(p. 12-93). Two basic types: the <b>rotary</b> inverter and the <b>static\n(solid-state)</b> inverter — the static one has no moving parts (p. 12-93).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Slip rings → AC; <b>commutator → DC</b>; coil + commutator =\n<b>armature</b> (p. 12-45, 12-125).</li>\n<li><b>Right-hand MOTOR rule:</b> index = field, second = current,\n<b>thumb = motion</b>; torque factors: field strength, turns, <b>coil\nposition</b> (p. 12-138).</li>\n<li>DC motors: <b>series</b> (high starting torque, starter, <b>overspeeds\nunloaded</b>), <b>shunt</b> (constant speed), <b>compound</b> (both windings)\n(p. 12-141/12-142).</li>\n<li><b>Counter emf</b> opposes applied voltage; speed ↑ → armature current ↓\n(p. 12-142).</li>\n<li>AC motors: <b>induction</b> (squirrel cage; runs just under <b>synchronous\nspeed</b>; the difference = <b>slip</b>) & <b>synchronous</b> (constant speed);\nAC-series motor runs on <b>AC or DC</b>; alternator frequency = poles + rotor\nspeed (p. 12-147…12-157).</li>\n<li>Voltage regulation = vary <b>field-circuit resistance</b>; vibrating-type →\n<b>carbon pile</b> for heavy duty; three-unit = voltage regulator + current\nlimiter + <b>reverse current cut-out</b> (prevents <b>motoring</b>)\n(p. 12-159…12-161).</li>\n<li>DC generator types: <b>series, shunt, compound</b>; series = poor\nregulation, never on airplanes (p. 12-132/12-133).</li>\n<li>Inverter: DC → AC, <b>400 cps</b>, rotary or <b>static</b> (p. 12-93).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Motors, generators, voltage regulation, inverters), pp. 12-45, 12-93, 12-125…12-161.</p>\n", "topics": ["Motors & Generators", "Voltage Regulation"], "quiz": [{"q": "As a DC motor's armature speed INCREASES, the counter emf increases and the current through the armature:", "options": ["Increases", "Reverses", "Decreases", "Stays the same"], "correct": 2, "short": "Decreases", "expl": "The faster the armature rotates, the greater the counter emf; counter emf opposes the applied voltage, so armature current decreases as speed increases. A motor draws high current on starting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "A DC motor that combines a series winding and a shunt winding in the field is a:", "options": ["Synchronous motor", "Shunt motor", "Series motor", "Compound motor"], "correct": 3, "short": "Compound motor", "expl": "The compound motor combines series and shunt motors; it has both a shunt winding and a series winding in the field. Its starting torque is higher than a shunt but lower than a series motor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "What is the danger if a SERIES motor has its load removed entirely?", "options": ["It draws no current", "It stalls immediately", "It may overspeed until the armature flies apart", "It reverses direction"], "correct": 2, "short": "It may overspeed and fly apart", "expl": "A series motor runs at high speed under light load; if the load is removed entirely, the motor may operate at such a high speed that the armature flies apart.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "The frequency of an alternator's output voltage depends on the number of poles and the:", "options": ["Voltage applied", "Speed of rotation of the rotor", "Core material", "Wire gauge"], "correct": 1, "short": "Speed of rotation", "expl": "Alternator frequency depends on the number of poles and the speed of rotation: the faster the speed (or the more poles), the higher the frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-157", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "The three units of a three-unit regulator used on many light aircraft are a voltage regulator, a current limiter, and a:", "options": ["Reverse current cut-out", "Field discharge diode", "Spike suppressor"], "correct": 0, "short": "Reverse current cut-out", "expl": "Three-unit regulator = (1) voltage regulator, (2) current regulator/limiter to cap generator output current, (3) reverse current cut-out to disconnect the battery from the generator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-161", "topic": "Voltage Regulation"}, {"q": "Aircraft inverters are usually built to supply AC current at a frequency of:", "options": ["1,000 cps", "60 cps", "400 cps", "50 cps"], "correct": 2, "short": "400 cps", "expl": "Inverters are usually built to supply current at a frequency of 400 cps. Some provide more than one voltage (e.g., 26 V AC and 115 V AC).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-93", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}, {"q": "Series-wound DC generators are never employed as airplane generators because they have:", "options": ["No residual magnetism", "Too little starting torque", "Very poor voltage regulation under changing load"], "correct": 2, "short": "Very poor voltage regulation under changing load", "expl": "In a series generator the load current IS the field current, so output voltage swings up and down with load. Airplane generators use shunt or compound field connections instead.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-132/12-133", "topic": "Voltage Regulation"}, {"q": "If the battery is not disconnected when generator voltage falls below battery voltage, the battery discharges through the generator armature and drives the generator like a motor. This action is called:", "options": ["Flashing the field", "Paralleling", "Motoring the generator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Motoring the generator", "expl": "That is exactly what the reverse current cut-out prevents: without it, the battery would 'motor' the generator and discharge itself in a short time.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-161", "topic": "Voltage Regulation"}, {"q": "A DC motor whose field winding is connected in PARALLEL (shunt) with the armature, giving fairly constant speed, is a:", "options": ["Series motor", "Shunt motor", "Stepper motor", "Universal motor"], "correct": 1, "short": "Shunt motor", "expl": "In a shunt motor the field winding is connected in parallel (shunt) with the armature; starting torque is less than a series motor's, but speed is more constant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-142", "topic": "Motors & Generators"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s5", "title": "Semiconductors, diodes & rectifiers", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> A semiconductor sits on the fence between conductor and\ninsulator — and by salting it with impurities we make materials that conduct\non command. Every radio, computer, and glass-cockpit box is built on this\ntrick, and it starts with one part: the diode, a one-way check valve for\ncurrent.</p>\n\n<h3>Semiconductor materials</h3>\n<p>The two most common semiconductor materials — each with exactly <b>four\nvalence electrons</b> — are <b>germanium and silicon</b> (p. 12-95). Their atoms\njoin by sharing electron pairs: the very stable <b>covalent bond</b> (p. 12-95).</p>\n<p><b>Doping</b> is adding small amounts of impurities to increase current flow\n(p. 12-96):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Donor</b> impurity (phosphorus, arsenic) → EXCESS electrons →\n<b>N-type</b> material (p. 12-96).</li>\n<li><b>Acceptor</b> impurity (boron, aluminum) → electron shortages called\n<b>\"holes\"</b> → <b>P-type</b> material (p. 12-96).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The PN junction — a diode</h3>\n<p>Join P to N and you get the <b>diode</b> — a two-element device that uses the\nrectifying property of the junction to <b>permit current in one direction\nonly</b> (p. 12-98). At the junction, electrons and holes cancel out in a thin\nlayer with no free carriers — a lack, or <i>depletion</i>, of them — so that\nlayer is the <b>depletion region</b>. The electrostatic field that builds\nacross the junction there is called the <b>barrier</b>, because carriers must\novercome it to cross (p. 12-98).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>FORWARD biased</b> = conducts easily (p. 12-98). Hookup: <b>negative\nsource to the N material, positive to the P material</b> — like pushes like\ncarriers toward the junction and collapses the depletion zone (p. 12-99).</li>\n<li><b>REVERSE biased</b> = blocks; the most noticeable effect is the depletion\nzone growing <b>WIDER</b> (p. 12-99).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Rectifiers — AC into DC</h3>\n<p>A <b>rectifier</b> transforms AC into DC by limiting/regulating the direction\nof current flow (p. 12-100). Two circuits to know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Half-wave:</b> one diode passes only one half (say, the positive swing)\nof each AC cycle — the other half is wasted (p. 12-101).</li>\n<li><b>Full-wave bridge:</b> <b>FOUR diodes</b> steer BOTH halves the same way —\noutput is a continuous sequence of pulses (p. 12-101). This is what lives in\nyour aircraft alternator.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Special diodes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Zener diode:</b> designed to break down (conduct) at a specific\nREVERSE-bias voltage — used as a <b>voltage regulator</b> (p. 12-103).</li>\n<li><b>LED — the light-emitting diode:</b> a forward-biased diode that\ndissipates its energy as <b>LIGHT</b> instead of heat (p. 12-103).</li>\n<li><b>Varactor (varicap):</b> a variable-CAPACITANCE diode — its capacitance is\ncontrolled by the applied reverse voltage (p. 12-104). (Electronic tuning.)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Semiconductors = <b>silicon & germanium</b> (4 valence electrons);\n<b>covalent bonds</b>; <b>doping</b> raises conduction (p. 12-95/12-96).</li>\n<li><b>Donor → N-type (extra electrons); acceptor → P-type (holes)</b> (p. 12-96).</li>\n<li>Diode = PN junction, one-way current; <b>depletion region</b> at the\njunction; <b>forward bias (− to N, + to P) conducts; reverse bias widens the\ndepletion zone</b> and blocks (p. 12-98/12-99).</li>\n<li><b>Rectifier = AC → DC</b>; half-wave = half the cycle; <b>full-wave bridge\n= 4 diodes</b>, continuous pulses (p. 12-100/12-101).</li>\n<li><b>Zener = reverse-breakdown voltage regulator; LED = light; varactor =\nvoltage-controlled capacitance</b> (p. 12-103/12-104).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Semiconductors, diodes, rectifiers), pp. 12-95…12-104.</p>\n", "topics": ["Semiconductors & Diodes"], "quiz": [{"q": "A semiconductor doped with a donor impurity (such as phosphorus or arsenic) that produces an EXCESS of electrons is called:", "options": ["Neutral-type", "Intrinsic", "P-type", "N-type"], "correct": 3, "short": "N-type", "expl": "An N-type semiconductor is doped with a donor impurity, producing an excess of electrons; the electrons are the majority current carriers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-96", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "A rectifier that produces output from only one half (e.g., the positive swing) of each AC cycle is a:", "options": ["Half-wave rectifier", "Full-wave rectifier", "Zener rectifier", "Bridge rectifier"], "correct": 0, "short": "Half-wave rectifier", "expl": "The half-wave rectifier reproduces the input minus the negative voltage swing — it uses only one half of each cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-101", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "In a forward-biased diode where energy is dissipated as LIGHT rather than heat, the device is a:", "options": ["Light-emitting diode (LED)", "Zener diode", "Varactor", "Photodiode"], "correct": 0, "short": "Light-emitting diode (LED)", "expl": "In an LED, the energy released as electrons fall into holes is dissipated as light. Advantages over incandescent lamps: longer life, lower voltage, faster operation, less heat.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-103", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "A two-element semiconductor device that uses the rectifying properties of a PN junction to permit current flow in one direction only is the:", "options": ["Diode (PN junction diode)", "Resistor", "Capacitor", "Transistor"], "correct": 0, "short": "Diode (PN junction diode)", "expl": "The semiconductor diode (PN junction diode) is a two-element device that converts AC into DC by permitting current flow in one direction only.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-98", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "The process of adding small amounts of impurities to a semiconductor material to increase its current flow is called:", "options": ["Rectifying", "Sintering", "Doping", "Biasing"], "correct": 2, "short": "Doping", "expl": "Doping is the process by which small amounts of additives (impurities) are added to a semiconductor to increase current flow by adding electrons or holes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-96", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "In a FORWARD biased diode, the negative voltage source connects to the N-type material and the positive source connects to the:", "options": ["Cathode", "P-type material", "Depletion zone", "N-type material"], "correct": 1, "short": "P-type material", "expl": "In a forward biased PN junction, the negative source connects to N-type and the positive source connects to P-type material, allowing current to flow easily.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-99", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "A variable-capacitance diode whose capacitance is controlled by the reverse voltage applied is a:", "options": ["Schottky", "Varactor (varicap)", "Zener", "LED"], "correct": 1, "short": "Varactor", "expl": "The varactor (varicap) is a variable-capacitance diode; the reverse voltage applied controls its capacitance. It often replaces mechanically tuned capacitors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-104", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "The very stable bond in which two silicon atoms each share one electron equally is called a:", "options": ["Metallic bond", "Hydrogen bond", "Ionic bond", "Covalent bond"], "correct": 3, "short": "Covalent bond", "expl": "Two silicon atoms share electrons equally in a covalent bond, which is very stable and holds the atoms together tightly.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-95", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}, {"q": "A diode designed to break down (conduct) at a specific reverse-bias voltage and act as a voltage regulator is a:", "options": ["Light-emitting diode", "Zener diode", "Schottky diode", "Photodiode"], "correct": 1, "short": "Zener diode", "expl": "Zener (breakdown) diodes conduct when reverse-bias voltage reaches a designed value, then behave like a constant voltage source — functioning as a voltage regulator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-103", "topic": "Semiconductors & Diodes"}]}, {"id": "b4m4s6", "title": "Transistors, logic gates & binary", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The Big Idea.</b> Put TWO junctions in one crystal and you get the\n<b>transistor</b> — a valve where a trickle controls a torrent. Wire millions\nof them as switches and you get logic gates, and logic gates speak a two-digit\nlanguage: binary. This section closes out the chapter — and the ACS list.</p>\n\n<h3>The transistor</h3>\n<p>A <b>three-terminal device used primarily to amplify signals and control\ncurrent</b> (p. 12-105). Its three regions: <b>collector, emitter, base</b>\n(p. 12-105) — and of the three, <b>the EMITTER is heavily doped</b> (it has to\nemit the carriers) (p. 12-105).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sandwich order gives the two families: <b>NPN or PNP — classified by the\narrangement of their N and P materials</b> (p. 12-105). On the schematic symbol\nyou tell them apart <b>by the direction of the arrow on the EMITTER</b>\n(p. 12-106) — NPN's arrow points out (\"Not Pointing iN\").</li>\n<li>In a PNP transistor the majority current carriers are <b>holes</b> (p. 12-107).</li>\n<li><b>Amplifier biasing:</b> emitter-base junction biased <b>FORWARD</b> (low\nresistance); collector-base junction biased <b>REVERSE</b> (high resistance)\n(p. 12-106). A small signal on the low-resistance side controls a big current\nthrough the high-resistance side — the name literally says it:\n<b>TRANSfer + resISTOR = transistor</b> (p. 12-107).</li>\n<li>The <b>field-effect transistor (FET)</b> has only ONE PN junction and is a\n<b>VOLTAGE-controlled</b> device (vs. current-controlled) (p. 12-107).</li>\n<li>Of the three amplifier configurations, the <b>common-emitter (CE)</b> gives\nthe best combined voltage + current gain and is the only one that inverts —\na <b>180-degree phase reversal</b> (p. 12-108).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Logic gates — transistors as switches</h3>\n<p>Digital circuits deal in 1s and 0s (high/low). The gates (pp. 12-121/12-122):</p>\n<table><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Output is HIGH (1) when…</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AND</b></td><td>ALL inputs are high</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>OR</b></td><td>ANY one (or more) input is high</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>NOT (inverter)</b></td><td>the input is LOW — it flips the state</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>NAND</b></td><td>NOT-AND: output LOW only when ALL inputs high</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>NOR</b></td><td>only when ALL inputs are LOW</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>X-OR (exclusive OR)</b></td><td>exactly ONE input is 1 (both 1 → 0)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>X-NOR</b></td><td>inputs MATCH: all 1s or all 0s</td></tr></table>\n<p>All these functions come packaged in the <b>integrated circuit (IC)</b>,\nwhose advantages over discrete parts are being <b>smaller, less power-hungry,\nvery reliable, and inexpensive</b> (p. 12-122).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">ACS note (AM.I.A.K22)</span>\nThe ACS also lists RAM, ROM, NVRAM, and flip-flops under digital logic. The\n-30B Ch.12 stops at gates and ICs — those memory/latch devices are covered\nin your AET Academy avionics modules. Short version: a flip-flop is a gate\npair that REMEMBERS one bit; RAM = read/write memory, ROM = permanent,\nNVRAM = keeps data with power off.</div>\n\n<h3>Binary numbers (ACS AM.I.A.K23; -30B Ch.3, pp. 3-27/3-28)</h3>\n<p>Why two digits? Because a transistor switch has exactly two states — OFF (0)\nand ON (1). Binary is a <b>Base-2</b> system using only <b>0 and 1</b>, with\nplace values based on <b>powers of 2</b>: reading right to left —\n1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 (p. 3-27).</p>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Both conversions, worked (handbook's own examples)</span>\n<b>Binary → decimal:</b> add the place values holding a 1. 10110011 =\n128 + 32 + 16 + 2 + 1 = <b>179</b> (p. 3-27/3-28).<br>\n<b>Decimal → binary:</b> subtract place values top-down. 233: −128 ✓, −64 ✓,\n−32 ✓, 16 ✗, −8 ✓, 4 ✗, 2 ✗, −1 ✓ → <b>11101001</b> (p. 3-28).</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Transistor: 3 terminals — <b>collector, emitter, base</b>; emitter =\n<b>heavily doped</b>; NPN/PNP by <b>N-P arrangement</b>, told apart by the\n<b>emitter arrow</b>; PNP carriers = <b>holes</b> (p. 12-105…12-107).</li>\n<li>Amplifier bias: <b>E-B forward, C-B reverse</b>; name = <b>transfer +\nresistor</b>; <b>FET = one junction, voltage-controlled</b>; <b>common-emitter =\nbest gain + 180° reversal</b> (p. 12-106…12-108).</li>\n<li>Gates: AND (all), OR (any), NOT/inverter (flip), NAND (low only when all\nhigh), <b>NOR (high only when all low)</b>, <b>X-OR (exactly one)</b>, X-NOR\n(match); IC = small/reliable/cheap/low-power (p. 12-121/12-122).</li>\n<li>Binary: Base-2, digits <b>0 and 1</b>, place values = <b>powers of 2</b>;\n<b>10110011 = 179; 233 = 11101001</b> (p. 3-27/3-28).</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p class=\"cite\">Source: FAA-H-8083-30B, Chapter 12 (Transistors, logic circuits), pp. 12-105…12-122; binary: FAA-H-8083-30B Ch.3, pp. 3-27/3-28.</p>\n", "topics": ["Transistors", "Logic Circuits", "Binary Numbers"], "quiz": [{"q": "A logic gate whose output is HIGH when ANY ONE (or more) of its inputs is high is the:", "options": ["AND gate", "OR gate", "Inverter", "NAND gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "OR gate", "expl": "The OR gate output is high whenever any one of its inputs is high; the output is low only when no input is high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "The name 'transistor' is a combination of which two words?", "options": ["Transit and register", "Transform and resistor", "Transmit and resistor", "Transfer and resistor"], "correct": 3, "short": "Transfer and resistor", "expl": "Because the device transfers current from a low-resistance path to a high-resistance one, its name combines 'transfer' and 'resistor' — transistor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-107", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "A logic gate whose output is HIGH only when ALL of its inputs are high is the:", "options": ["NOT gate", "NOR gate", "OR gate", "AND gate"], "correct": 3, "short": "AND gate", "expl": "The AND gate output is high only when all inputs are high. If any input is low, the output is low — it determines when conditions are met at the same time.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "To use a transistor as an amplifier, the emitter-base junction is biased in the forward direction (low resistance) and the collector-base junction is:", "options": ["Left unbiased", "Forward biased (low resistance)", "Reverse biased (high resistance)", "Shorted"], "correct": 2, "short": "Reverse biased (high resistance)", "expl": "For amplification, the emitter-base junction is forward biased (low resistance) and the collector-base junction is reverse biased (high resistance), producing power gain.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-106", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "Transistors are classified as either NPN or PNP according to:", "options": ["The arrangement of their N and P materials", "The number of leads", "Their color code", "Their power rating"], "correct": 0, "short": "Arrangement of N and P materials", "expl": "Transistors are classified NPN or PNP according to the arrangement of their N and P materials. NPN has P sandwiched between two N regions; PNP is the opposite.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-105", "topic": "Transistors"}, {"q": "A logic gate whose output is HIGH only when ALL of its inputs are LOW is the:", "options": ["AND gate", "NOR gate", "NAND gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "NOR gate", "expl": "The NOR gate is an OR with an inverted output: any high input drives the output low; only all-low inputs give a high output — the exact opposite of the OR gate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-122", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "A logic gate (also called a NOT gate) that converts one logic state into its opposite is the:", "options": ["OR gate", "Inverter", "NAND gate", "AND gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "Inverter (NOT gate)", "expl": "The inverter (NOT gate) converts one logic state into the opposite — a 1 to a 0 or a 0 to a 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 12-121", "topic": "Logic Circuits"}, {"q": "Using the handbook's method, the binary number 10110011 converts to what decimal number?", "options": ["179", "163", "187"], "correct": 0, "short": "179", "expl": "Add the place values holding a 1: 128 + 32 + 16 + 2 + 1 = 179. (Ignore the places holding a 0.) This is the handbook's own worked example.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 3-27/3-28", "topic": "Binary Numbers"}, {"q": "The binary number system is a base-2 system that uses which digits?", "options": ["1 and 2", "0 through 9", "0 and 1"], "correct": 2, "short": "0 and 1", "expl": "Binary has only two digits, 0 and 1 — matching a switch or transistor that is either OFF (0) or ON (1). That is why aircraft digital electronics are built on binary.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-30B p. 3-27", "topic": "Binary Numbers"}]}]}]};
